\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says his hands are tied on a $15 minimum wage. That's not true\b0\par\par\uc2 David Sirota \par \uc2 1,313 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 March 2021\par 13:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats argue that it\u8217\'20\'19s impossible to deliver a higher minimum wage, when there are plenty of ways to overcome this impasse\par \par \uc2 When a Republican is president, Democratic politicians, pundits and activists will tell you that the presidency is an all-powerful office that can do anything it wants. When a Democrat is president, these same politicians, pundits and activists will tell you that the presidency has no power to do anything. In fact, they will tell you a Democratic president cannot even use the bully pulpit and other forms of pressure to try to shift the votes of senators in his own party.\par \par \uc2 A tale from history proves this latter myth is complete garbage \u8211\'20\'13 and that tale is newly relevant in today\u8217\'20\'19s supercharged debate over a $15 minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 In that debate so far, we have seen Democratic senators prepare to surrender the $15 minimum wage their party {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wwlp.com/news/biden-pushes-for-increasing-minimum-wage-to-15-an-hour/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 promised}}}\uc2  by insisting they are powerless in the face of a non-binding advisory opinion of a parliamentarian they can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailyposter.com/p/breaking-secret-memo-shows-how-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ignore}}}\uc2  or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2021/02/26/senate-parliamentarian-minimum-wage-overrule/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fire}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That explanation is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/davidsirota/status/1366107871419621377?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 patently ridiculous}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailyposter.com/p/breaking-secret-memo-shows-how-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 factually false}}}\uc2 , so Democratic apologists are starting to further justify the surrender by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/billscher/status/1365016024207269890?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggesting}}}\uc2  that even if the party kept a $15 minimum wage in the Covid relief bill, conservative Democrats such as Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  would block it anyway.\par \par \uc2 The White House itself is now falling back on the idea that it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jeneps/status/1365098078529675264?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doesn\u8217\'20\'19t}}}\uc2  have the votes to do much of anything, insinuating that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who occupies the world\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful office \u8211\'20\'13 somehow has no power to try to change the legislative dynamic. And this spin is being predictably amplified {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JamesSurowiecki/status/1364205145064697858"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 across}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/drvolts/status/1365759988342300672"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 social media}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To be sure, there is no guarantee that Manchin or Sinema could be moved. Maybe they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t, but maybe they could, considering they have both {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/story/2014/02/joe-manchin-minimum-wage-104056"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previously}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/113th-congress/house-bill/1010/cosponsors?searchResultViewType=expanded"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supported}}}\uc2  bills to increase the minimum wage. And we know they may be sensitive to pressure. After all, Manchin recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2021/02/03/joe-manchin-and-kamala-harris-sparred-over-west-virginia-interviews/4370364001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 freaked out}}}\uc2  and whined that \u8220\'20\'1Cno one called me\u8221\'20\'1D when Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  dared to do one straightforward interview with a West Virginia television station.\par \par \uc2 Whether such pressure ultimately works, the point is indisputable: it is laughable and preposterous to argue that a newly elected president has zero power to even try to shift the dynamic.\par \par \uc2 And yet, whether you call this all deliberate deception or learned helplessness, this fantastical myth of the Powerless President will inevitably be used to shield Biden from criticism for abandoning his pledge to fight for a $15 minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 The apologism is particularly absurd because unlike his predecessor \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who was a relative newcomer to politics, Biden\u8217\'20\'19s major selling point was that he knows \u8220\'20\'1Chow to make government work\u8221\'20\'1D. The guy explicitly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mcclatchydc.com/news/politics-government/election/campaigns/article232013502.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pitched}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2019/05/24/726433232/biden-sanders-highlight-familiar-split-in-democratic-party"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 himself}}}\uc2  as the best Democratic presidential candidate by suggesting that in an era of gridlock, he knows how to make the Democratic agenda a reality and Get Things Done\u8482\'21\'22, like master of the Senate Lyndon Baines Johnson.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s where LBJ himself comes in to destroy the narrative that Democratic presidents in general \u8211\'20\'13 and Biden specifically \u8211\'20\'13 are inherently helpless.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18\par \par \uc2 Lyndon\par \par \uc2 told\par \par \uc2 me\par \par \uc2 to\par \par \uc2 \u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In 1964, Johnson was trying to pass Medicare, but two conservative Democratic senators threatened to take down the entire legislation over a tax issue. In a story {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/stephaniekelton/status/1365742559100870661"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flagged}}}\uc2  by economist \uc2 Stephanie Kelton\uc2 , the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/VZcCCctpRQ?amp=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2  noted that months before that legislation passed: \u8220\'20\'1COpponents proposed a large and popular increase in Social Security benefits (and taxes) which would have made passage of new Medicare taxes almost impossible. At the last minute, Senators George Smathers of Florida and Russell Long of Louisiana, both Democrats but Medicare opponents, switched and voted to save Medicare. \u8216\'20\'18Lyndon told me to,\u8217\'20\'19 Senator Smathers explained.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The pivotal story was recounted in more detail in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://books.google.com/books?id=UEGLO9YUecEC&pg=PA6&lpg=PA6&dq=%22Johnson+knew+there+was+an+indispensable+role+that+only+he+could+play:+he+could+best+publicize+the+idea%22&source=bl&ots=celrkqpzKx&sig=ACfU3U2kW0pQH0B_07PTowCfjjsqWHXKEw&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiXt_6fm4vvAhWHqp4KHSj2DLkQ6AEwAHoECAMQAw#v=onepage&q=%E2%80%9CLyndon%20told%20me%20to%22&f=false"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Heart Of Power}}}\uc2  by \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s David Blumenthal (a former Obama administration official) and \uc2 Brown University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s James Morone. They noted that presidents can play a particularly unique role in these situations, and they warn against presidents who refuse to leverage their offices to push their agendas:\par \par \uc2 Johnson knew there was an indispensable role that only he could play: he could best publicize the idea, build support, jawbone interest groups into line, and organize (and lobby) the congressional coalition. When reporters asked Senator George Smathers (D-FL) why he had switched his vote and salvaged the administration\u8217\'20\'19s Medicare proposal in 1964, he responded, simply, \u8220\'20\'1CLyndon told me to.\u8221\'20\'1D Presidents win complicated reforms by doing what the office of the presidency is uniquely designed for \u8211\'20\'13 publicizing and persuading \u8230\'20\'26 There is, of course, a danger at the other extreme \u8211\'20\'13 that of the disengaged executive. The president chooses his analysts, gives them directions, and decides when the debate is over. The staff always knows when the boss has lost interest \u8211\'20\'13 and the issue, no matter how well staffed, is probably doomed.\par \par \uc2 There has been a lot of dishonesty and deception floating around Democratic Washington these days. There was the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailyposter.com/p/read-my-lips-2000-now"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lie}}}\uc2  two months ago that $2,000 checks would be coming \u8220\'20\'1Cimmediately\u8221\'20\'1D to a desperate nation struggling through a pandemic. There is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailyposter.com/p/breaking-secret-memo-shows-how-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lie}}}\uc2  about the parliamentarian supposedly being the reason the $15 minimum wage is stalled. There is once again the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailyposter.com/p/dems-gift-health-insurance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lie}}}\uc2  of a forthcoming \u8220\'20\'1Cpublic option\u8221\'20\'1D, which Democrats promised but which is barely being discussed at all, and is not part of the Covid relief legislation.\par \par \uc2 But the LBJ story shows that the mendacious narrative of a helpless Democratic president is the most pernicious lie of all.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18\par \par \uc2 Fighting\par \par \uc2 our\par \par \uc2 guts\par \par \uc2 out\par \par \uc2 \u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 v\par \par \uc2 pre-emptive\par \par \uc2 surrender\par \par \uc2 If this lie about a Powerless President seems familiar, that\u8217\'20\'19s because it was trotted out during the last Democratic presidency, when \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  refused to lift a finger to pressure similarly conservative Democratic senators to support a wildly popular public insurance option or a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/abandoning-efca-is-obamas_b_414209"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 union card check initiative}}}\uc2  that he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hrmorning.com/articles/obama-to-afl-cio-we-will-pass-efca/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 explicitly}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2009/dec/23/barack-obama/public-option-obama-platform/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 promised}}}\uc2 . He had enormous congressional majorities and a huge election mandate, but didn\u8217\'20\'19t bother to go to Democratic states to build Democratic voter pressure against recalcitrant Democratic senators.\par \par \uc2 On the contrary, Obama\u8217\'20\'19s chief of staff {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/WN/rahm-emanuel-retarded-comment-puts-offensiveness-spotlight/story?id=9738134"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 berated}}}\uc2  progressives trying to pressure conservative Democrats over health care reform and Obama simply {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2010/02/why-obama-dropped-the-public-option/346546/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surrendered}}}\uc2 . Meanwhile, obsequious liberal pundits scoffed at a so-called \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/2014/5/20/5732208/the-green-lantern-theory-of-the-presidency-explained"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Green Lantern Theory}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, mocking those who suggested that the most powerful man on Earth has any power to influence elected officials in his own party. Obama is still {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2020/12/in-conversation-with-barack-obama.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pretending}}}\uc2  he couldn\u8217\'20\'19t do anything.\par \par \uc2 Now we see this same Powerless President narrative in the minimum wage fight \u8211\'20\'13 and if you look closely, the Biden administration is all but admitting it\u8217\'20\'19s a lie.\par \par \uc2 After all, the White House {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/newsletters/playbook/2021/02/28/fears-about-trump-out-of-the-cage-491927"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 continues}}}\uc2  to say it is \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msnbc.com/the-reidout/watch/ron-klain-we-re-fighting-our-guts-out-to-get-neera-tanden-confirmed-for-omb-101545029762"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fighting our guts out}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for Neera Tanden\u8217\'20\'19s nomination, even though it might not have enough Senate votes for her confirmation. And yet, the same White House is simultaneously retreating on the minimum wage, seemingly unwilling to force a floor vote on the issue, even though presidential pressure, legislative brinkmanship, and negotiation could change the outcome.\par \par \uc2 In the Tanden situation, in fact, the Biden team is acting like a White House\u8217\'20\'19s power of persuasion and legislative arm twisting can potentially move votes for something a president cares about \u8211\'20\'13 in this case, the nomination of a Washington insider to a fancy White House job.\par \par \uc2 The real story, then, is that Biden seems unwilling to use the same influence to push as hard as possible for a minimum wage increase that would boost the pay of millions of Americans during an economic emergency.\par \par \uc2 He has the power to at least try \u8211\'20\'13 he just seems unwilling to.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 David Sirota is a Guardian US columnist and an award-winning investigative journalist. He is an editor at large at Jacobin, and the founder of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailyposter.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Daily Poster}}}\uc2 . He served as Bernie Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 presidential campaign speechwriter\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210301eh31001pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
