\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 A $15 minimum wage isn't just about justice. It's good economics\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,182 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2021\par 17:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The US has the lowest minimum wage of all G7 nations, in terms of purchasing power, and the third-highest poverty rate in the \uc2 OECD\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s one important aspect of the fight for a $15 minimum wage that is little understood: the fight isn\u8217\'20\'19t so much about raising pay for a few million workers. Rather it\u8217\'20\'19s about the far more ambitious goal of putting the US economy on a higher road, on a different track from being a low-wage economy. In Europe, many people scoff at the US as a country of low-wage McJobs with paltry benefits \u8211\'20\'13 often no paid sick days, no paid vacation and no health insurance. In Denmark, a \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  hamburger flipper {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/08/opinion/sunday/us-denmark-economy.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 averages $22 an hour}}}\uc2  (with six weeks\u8217\'20\'19 paid vacation), while in the US, fast-food jobs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes353023.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pay half that}}}\uc2  on average.\par \par \uc2 You might wonder: how can the United States, the world\u8217\'20\'19s wealthiest nation, be a low-wage economy? Of the 37 nations in the \uc2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\uc2 , the unofficial club of rich and near-rich nations, the US has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://stats.oecd.org/Index.aspx?QueryId=64193"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the third-highest percentage}}}\uc2  of low-wage workers, with nearly one in four workers defined as low-wage. Only Latvia and Romania are worse. (That study defines low-wage as earning less than two-thirds of a nation\u8217\'20\'19s median wage.) In another study, Brookings found that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/11/201911_Brookings-Metro_Pressrelease_lowwageworkforce.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 53 million Americans}}}\uc2  hold low-wage jobs, with a median pay of $10.22 an hour and median annual earnings of $17,950.\par \par \uc2 The US also has the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://stats.oecd.org/Index.aspx?QueryId=64193"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lowest minimum wage}}}\uc2  among the G7 industrial nations in terms of purchasing power. America\u8217\'20\'19s $7.25-an-hour federal minimum is 38% lower than Germany\u8217\'20\'19s and 30% lower than Britain\u8217\'20\'19s, Canada\u8217\'20\'19s and France\u8217\'20\'19s. This helps explain why the US has among {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://data.oecd.org/inequality/income-inequality.htm#indicator-chart"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the worst income inequality}}}\uc2  of the 37 \uc2 OECD\uc2  nations \u8211\'20\'13 only Mexico, Chile, Costa Rica and Bulgaria have greater inequality. And the US has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://data.oecd.org/inequality/poverty-rate.htm#indicator-chart"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the third highest poverty rate}}}\uc2  ; only Hungary and Costa Rica are worse.\par \par \uc2 The US didn\u8217\'20\'19t always have a low-road economy. In the 1950s, 1960s and 1970s, it had a high-road economy: labor unions were at their strongest, American businesses were booming (for the most part), and corporations shared their profits and prosperity with their workers as never before, helping build the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest and richest middle class. But beginning in the 1980s, many corporations pushed the US economy and American workers on to a lower road, as corporate America felt the sting of global competition, as Wall Street pushed companies ever harder to maximize profits, as labor unions grew weaker and as President Reagan and other Republicans weakened worker protections and did little to raise the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 The US also has the lowest minimum wage among the G7 industrial nations in terms of purchasing power\par \par \uc2 Because of persistent Republican opposition, the federal minimum wage hasn\u8217\'20\'19t increased since July 2009 \u8211\'20\'13 the longest stretch without an increase since Congress first enacted a minimum wage in 1938. Franklin Roosevelt signed the minimum wage into law during the Great Depression because he thought workers had far too little bargaining power and consumers far too little purchasing power. Roosevelt urged companies to pay fair wages, saying, \u8220\'20\'1CNo business which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://docs.fdrlibrary.marist.edu/odnirast.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 depends for existence on paying less than living wages}}}\uc2  to its workers has any right to continue in this country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s federal minimum wage is just $7.25 an hour \u8211\'20\'13 ?try maintaining a decent living at that pay level. The minimum wage is down 18% since it was last raised in 2009, after factoring in inflation, and down over 30%, after inflation, since 1968, during Lyndon Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s Great Society years.\par \par \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s business community has staunchly opposed any minimum wage increases, warning that they would push up prices and force some companies out of business. Those leading the charge against a higher minimum \u8211\'20\'13 corporate executives and well-paid lobbyists and thinktank fellows \u8211\'20\'13 repeatedly say that raising the wage floor would be a job killer. They note that the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  (CBO) recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbo.gov/system/files/2021-02/56975-Minimum-Wage.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated}}}\uc2  that increasing the minimum to $15 by 2025 would reduce employment by 1.4 million, although the treasury secretary, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , echoing many economic studies, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/markets/yellen-says-raising-minimum-wage-to-dollar15-an-hour-would-have-minimal-impact-on-jobs-but-congressional-budget-office-disagrees/ar-BB1cXOtz?li=BBnb7Kz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  a $15 minimum\u8217\'20\'19s effect on jobs would be \u8220\'20\'1Cvery minimal, if anything\u8221\'20\'1D. The CBO also predicted that increasing the minimum to $15 would raise the pay of 27 million workers nationwide and increase worker pay overall by $333bn \u8211\'20\'13 a step that would stimulate the struggling economy.\par \par \uc2 Corporations, along with their Republican allies, overwhelmingly oppose a $15 minimum; in doing so, however, they ignore the will of the vast majority of Americans. According to a Pew poll, Americans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2019/07/30/two-thirds-of-americans-favor-raising-federal-minimum-wage-to-15-an-hour/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 favor a $15 minimum by 67% to 33%}}}\uc2 . While low-wage workers would be most vulnerable to any job losses caused by a higher minimum, lower-income Americans shows even greater support for a $15 minimum. Pew found that 74% of Americans making under $40,000 a year support a $15 minimum wage, as do 56% of Republicans making under $40,000. Last November, Floridians \u8211\'20\'13 even as their state went for Trump \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2020/11/04/florida-amendment-2-minimum-wage/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted 61% to 39%}}}\uc2  in favor of raising their state\u8217\'20\'19s minimum to $15, joining eight other states that have approved a $15 minimum.\par \par \uc2 Despite such strong public backing for a $15 minimum, it looks doubtful that even one Republican senator \u8211\'20\'13 even though the Republican party now describes itself as the party of workers \u8211\'20\'13 will vote for a $15 minimum. President Biden has championed a $15 minimum: \u8220\'20\'1CNo matter where you work in America, if you work full time or 40 hours a week, you should not live in poverty,\u8221\'20\'1D he has said. \u8220\'20\'1CA $15 minimum wage accomplishes that.\u8221\'20\'1D A full-time worker making $7.25 an hour earns $15,080 a year; in contrast, a full-time worker earning $15 will make $31,200. In the face of opposition from Republicans and some congressional Democrats, however, Biden has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/us-policy/2021/02/16/covid-relief-biden/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hinted}}}\uc2  he might compromise on a lower number.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , a progressive research group, predicts that if the minimum rises to $15, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/why-america-needs-a-15-minimum-wage/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly one-third}}}\uc2  (31%) of African Americans and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/why-america-needs-a-15-minimum-wage/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one-quarter}}}\uc2  (26%) of Latinos will receive a pay increase. Those pushing for a $15 minimum are carrying on a fight that Martin Luther King Jr, championed: the fight for decent pay and dignity at work. In 1967, the year before he died while fighting for higher wages for sanitation workers in Memphis, King {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.beaconbroadside.com/broadside/2017/06/martin-luther-king-jrs-where-do-we-go-from-here-turns-50.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1CThere is nothing but a lack of social vision to prevent us from paying an adequate wage to every American whether he [or she] is a hospital worker, laundry worker, maid or day laborer.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Millions of low-wage workers are still waiting for America to heed King\u8217\'20\'19s social vision.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Steven Greenhouse is a journalist and author, focusing on labor and the workplace. He is the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/246798/beaten-down-worked-up-by-steven-greenhouse/9781101874431/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Beaten Down, Worked Up: The Past, Present, and Future of American Labor}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210226eh2q001js\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
