\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 'I thought it was going to break me': Covid's brutal toll on working mothers\b0\par\par\uc2 Katherine Goldstein \par \uc2 3,061 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2021\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Mothers who have been laid off, pushed out or unable to find work describe battling circumstances far beyond their control\par \par \uc2 Throughout the last year, we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen an endless stream of depressing statistics about the economic impact Covid has had on women. The toll Covid has had on working women is breathtaking. Over 2 million women are no longer in the labor force at all , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/02/08/womens-labor-force-participation-rate-hit-33-year-low-in-january-2021.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reverting to 1988 levels of labor participation for women in the US }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a generation of progress erased in a year. Even if 2021 sees a successful turnaround on the virus, without targeted public policy changes, it will take until 2030 to climb back to 2019 employment levels for women, according to gender economist Katica Roy.\par \par \uc2 Some of the hardest hit are mothers, who are more likely to earn less than childless women and fathers, and who have often taken on more caregiving responsibilities. Women of color also faced wage gaps before the pandemic and are more likely to work in the service sector and low-wage jobs \u8211\'20\'13 brutally hard-hit industries.\par \par \uc2 As we near the first anniversary of the Covid shutdowns we put faces to these large, faceless statistics. These are the stories of five mothers with young children who have been laid off, pushed out, unable to find work \u8211\'20\'13 battling circumstances far beyond their control as they work to keep their families afloat.\par \par \uc2 Caliana Mu\u241\'00\'F1oz,\par \par \uc2 24, former lead VIP Performance Venue host, currently a clerk at shipping container company\par \par \uc2 in New Orleans, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Single mom to a four-year-old.\par \par \uc2 What Caliana expected to earn in 2020 : $20-25,000.\par \par \uc2 How much the pandemic has cost her: $20,000 in lost income and interest on credit card debt.\par \par \uc2 What have you lost, beyond money? My innocence. I thought there was no way the government wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t extend the federal unemployment benefits this summer. When I realized I would only be getting $91 a week in unemployment from the state, it was a moment of panic.\par \par \uc2 Caliana\u8217\'20\'19s story: At the start of 2020, I was a VIP host at a new music venue. It was really promising and exciting. It was part-time and my hours were a little erratic, but I felt confident it would turn into a full-time job.\par \par \uc2 When everything closed, I really appreciated the time because it was just me and my daughter. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have work. She didn\u8217\'20\'19t have school. Every day was a new adventure. The first stimulus really saved me, but my biggest saving grace is that I own a home. It was passed down to me from a relative and it was paid off. I don\u8217\'20\'19t pay rent, but I do incur other expenses, like property taxes, utilities, repair costs.\par \par \uc2 Not knowing what would happen to my music venue job, I looked for other paying work. I did sporadic shifts as an exotic dancer at a Gentlemen\u8217\'20\'19s club, but I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t too comfortable with it. It gave me body image issues, and I felt like I was doing it out of desperation. The dancers wore masks and the patrons were supposed to wear masks, but many didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to. The venue had to close at 11pm, so the money wasn\u8217\'20\'19t very good \u8211\'20\'13 I\u8217\'20\'19d make maybe $100 or $150 a night.\par \par \uc2 I found a part-time job with the census, but by the fall I was going into credit card debt to pay for food. I probably should have applied for food stamps, but didn\u8217\'20\'19t think of it. I was moving to payment plans on all my bills because I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t pay the full amount.\par \par \uc2 I remember one day crying, thinking, what am I going to do? I responded to a \uc2 Linkedin\uc2  ad \u8211\'20\'13 and found the job I have now. I never would have imagined working for an international shipping company.\par \par \uc2 After I started my new job, I heard at the end of 2020 about how 12 million people could lose their unemployment benefits if Congress didn\u8217\'20\'19t act, and I just started crying because I felt so grateful for this opportunity. Maybe I\u8217\'20\'19ll be able to grow my career or live in another country someday. I\u8217\'20\'19ve already gotten one raise, and I now make $39,000 a year, plus health insurance and benefits. Much more than I made as a VIP host. It\u8217\'20\'19s just the best thing that\u8217\'20\'19s been able to happen to me during the pandemic, to be honest.\par \par \uc2 Elisa Cardnell,\par \par \uc2 34,\par \par \uc2 navy veteran, former congressional candidate, political consultant, executive director of Moms in Office.\par \par \uc2 Divorced mom to a 10-year-old in Houston, Texas\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What Elisa expected to earn in 2020 : $60,000.\par \par \uc2 How much the pandemic has cost her : $50,000 (lost salary, savings and retirement fund liquidation, investments in her new businesses).\par \par \uc2 What have you lost, beyond money? : The extrovert in me has been struggling so much this last year with not seeing people and not having that social interaction that was abundant on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 When 2020 started, I was in the midst of a congressional campaign. My life was 100 miles an hour, and I was away from home from probably 7.30am to 10pm most nights. Life was busy and I was good financially. I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t rich, but I was steady and stable, which is what you expect from someone who\u8217\'20\'19s in their mid-30s and working. We were actually one of the first campaigns in the country to hire a nanny on staff because as a single mom \u8211\'20\'13 I was a teacher before I ran for office \u8211\'20\'13 I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have the resources to pay out of my own pocket. I saw my daughter as much as I could, and she knew I was going to go do things to change the world.\par \par \uc2 We forced a runoff in the Democratic primary, but I was running against a better funded candidate. We announced the decision on March 9th to end the campaign. That was Monday, and by Friday, I was a stay-at-home mom with a kid doing virtual school for the rest of third grade. I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t take a salary from the campaign any more, so I was reliant on my VA disability income \u8211\'20\'13 I\u8217\'20\'19m an 80% disabled veteran through the VA.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m very fortunate, even without a steady full-time job, to have had access to good healthcare through the VA\par \par \uc2 It took until late April to get unemployment. The system wasn\u8217\'20\'19t designed for this level of unemployment. While I waited, I tapped out my mutual fund - I had a little bit of a Roth IRA and I pulled every penny I could to make it through those couple of months. But once unemployment kicked in, the federal benefit of $600 a week meant I was making ends meet. One of the things I figured was after I ran for Congress, there would be opportunities open to me outside of the classroom. I put out applications but, to be honest, I don\u8217\'20\'19t think a lot of people were hiring, even when they had job postings listed.\par \par \uc2 When the $600 federal unemployment ran out this summer \u8211\'20\'13 I put my mortgage into forbearance, I had the loan through the VA , but I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford to pay it. There was a time where my bank account hovered around zero.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m very fortunate, even without a steady full-time job, to have had access to good healthcare through the VA \u8211\'20\'13 the vast majority of Americans don\u8217\'20\'19t have this when they are between jobs.\par \par \uc2 I am still committed to helping my community. I used my stimulus checks to start two different businesses \u8211\'20\'13 one to help with virtual learning and one to get more working-class people running for office.\par \par \uc2 Andrea Rhymes, 27,\par \par \uc2 flight a\par \par \uc2 ttendant and photographer in Indianapolis, Indiana\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Partnered and 36 weeks pregnant (at time of interview).\par \par \uc2 How much she expected to earn in 2020: $45,000.\par \par \uc2 How much the pandemic has cost her: $30,000 (in lost salary and earnings expected from her photography business).\par \par \uc2 Where do you see yourself in two years? Running and owning my photography business, a studio, full time.\par \par \uc2 Andrea\u8217\'20\'19s story : In January 2020, I received my \u8220\'20\'1Cline\u8221\'20\'1D as a flight attendant \u8211\'20\'13 my schedule for the following month. With that comes more money and freedom. I was planning to invest in my photography business. I started shooting professionally two years ago, but have been a photographer since 2009.\par \par \uc2 When the pandemic hit, I was still flying, but things changed a lot. In early April, I remember going through three major airport hubs and it felt like a ghost town. I was operating flights with 10 people consistently, and I even operated a flight with one passenger. My airline didn\u8217\'20\'19t shut down completely, but through April and May I got maybe four calls throughout both months. I was home a lot, and so was my boyfriend.\par \par \uc2 In June, I realized I might be pregnant. I kept working throughout the summer, but the job got harder. The airline clientele changed a lot. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t have a lot of business travellers any more, and because the airlines lowered their prices so much, we had a lot of people who were first-time flyers. Some of them looked for loopholes to get out of wearing a mask, like saying \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m eating!\u8221\'20\'1D during the whole flight. One passenger was asked twice to put his mask on, and when I asked him a third time, he yelled: \u8220\'20\'1CI shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to listen to a 5ft-nothing Black woman. Don\u8217\'20\'19t you know who I am?\u8221\'20\'1D He jumped up and other passengers held him back from rushing me. I was about 16 weeks pregnant at the time, and all I could think about was protecting my baby. That was my last flight.\par \par \uc2 After that I took a voluntary unpaid leave. I applied for food stamps and unemployment. I\u8217\'20\'19ve never shied away from asking for help and right now the country is crying for help. But I\u8217\'20\'19ve been employed since I was 16, so I don\u8217\'20\'19t like sitting idle. I had to force myself to create nine-to-five office hours and be a business woman for my photography business. I was able to get three to four bookings a week during the warmer months, and my community really rallied around me, as a Black-owned business.\par \par \uc2 Jenna Mason,\par \par \uc2 39, writer, editor and waitress. Oxford, Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Divorced mom to two kids, 13 and 10.\par \par \uc2 What Jenna expected to earn in 2020: $32,000.\par \par \uc2 How much the pandemic has cost her: $25,000 (in lost income, bill payment late fees and moving costs).\par \par \uc2 What have you lost, beyond money? Confidence in myself. I\u8217\'20\'19ve lost the image of myself as a strong, independent, self-sufficient woman and mother.\par \par \uc2 Jenna\u8217\'20\'19s story: My primary work is as a writer and editor at a non-profit, but I\u8217\'20\'19ve worked in restaurants on and off for about 20 years, picking up shifts to earn extra money.\par \par \uc2 At the start of 2020, my non-profit job cut my hours in half , so I hustled to get more waitressing gigs to help with medical bills (I don\u8217\'20\'19t have health insurance and broke my kneecap in the summer of 2019.) Then everything shut down. The timing was terrible for our college town. When they cancelled the first home baseball series, everyone working in restaurants was like, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe are really screwed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I got federal and state unemployment for about four weeks, but I went back to work when restaurants reopened, so I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t eligible. I definitely wasn\u8217\'20\'19t earning enough to get by with 25% capacity dining rooms and doing curbside pickup.\par \par \uc2 I was diagnosed with depression years ago, which I\u8217\'20\'19ve taken medication for. Over the summer, the stress of not earning enough money and fear of catching Covid sent my anxiety through the roof. I was slower and forgetful in ways that don\u8217\'20\'19t work well in restaurants. I even had to leave shifts early.\par \par \uc2 It feels good to help other people and it feels awful to have them help you\par \par \uc2 A new prescription helped, but when it ran out I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford a refill. It was much more expensive than my previous one, moving from a few dollars to $80 a month. I got let go from my two waitressing jobs, so I\u8217\'20\'19m back to just doing the 15 hours a week at the non-profit. I\u8217\'20\'19ve had to borrow from friends and family.\par \par \uc2 I grew up Christian. There\u8217\'20\'19s a Bible verse, \u8220\'20\'1CIt is more blessed to give than to receive.\u8221\'20\'1D Now I really have a totally different understanding of that Bible verse. It feels good to help other people and it feels awful to have them help you.\par \par \uc2 My rock bottom moment came on December 5th. We came home and my 13-year-old son found an eviction notice on the front door. I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have wanted him to see that. I was half a month behind on the rent, but I\u8217\'20\'19d spoken with the landlord and thought we had an understanding. Instead of moving in January, I was given three days to move. Since the eviction, my kids have been living with their dad full time. Until I can save up and find my own place, I\u8217\'20\'19ve been staying in a friend\u8217\'20\'19s extra bedroom, rent-free.\par \par \uc2 One silver lining is the outpouring of support from friends and family. There were a lot of times where I really thought this was going to break me and it hasn\u8217\'20\'19t yet.\par \par \uc2 Shana Thomas, 42, former store manager.\par \par \uc2 Currently an in-home caregiver for her youngest daughter, and a virtual learning assistant (unpaid) for her two oldest children.\par \par \uc2 Married with three kids, 13, 10, and\par \par \uc2 six.\par \par \uc2 What Shana expected to earn in 2020 : $93,000.\par \par \uc2 What the pandemic has cost her : between $60,000-70,000 including lost salary and bonuses, retirement contributions, unexpected medical deductibles and bills from losing her employer provided health insurance, and moving costs.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see yourself in two years? : I hope we are finally unpacked from our move, and we are getting the girls outside, camping, hiking, doing the things that we love and not looking back.\par \par \uc2 Our youngest daughter Bridget is a thriving kindergartener, but she has a lot of needs. She\u8217\'20\'19s cognitively typical, but she is partially paralyzed and is a wheelchair user \u8211\'20\'13 she requires a lot of therapies. When the shutdowns happened, my work ramped up and that really impacted the family.\par \par \uc2 My oldest was fine, but my middle daughter would spend 10 hours a day struggling with her assignments while I was at work \u8211\'20\'13 a nightmare for her. Preschool basically went away for Bridget and all of her therapies stopped. My husband was working full-time from home, and it was just impossible to split his time between his own job, helping our middle daughter with school and taking care of Bridget. It was a really tough situation.\par \par \uc2 There was a day this summer my husband and I finally reflected on how horrible this spring was for the two younger girls and what that would look like if things weren\u8217\'20\'19t back to normal in August. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t see any possibility of things being back to normal by August.\par \par \uc2 We looked at options. We were renting a home in a very nice area in Santa Clarita in LA county \u8211\'20\'13 the schools were great. We couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford to live there on one income. My grandparents\u8217\'20\'19 house in San Diego had been vacant for a year and a half, next door to my parents, and my dad had told us on multiple occasions, \u8220\'20\'1CHey, there\u8217\'20\'19s a house for you here if you need it, if something ever happened.\u8221\'20\'1D We decided the best thing for the family would be to move to San Diego. We\u8217\'20\'19re very lucky to have this opportunity. We are paying taxes, and utilities and are working on buying the house \u8211\'20\'13 but the costs are much lower than our rent in LA.\par \par \uc2 Even though I earned a little more than my husband with my end-of-the-year bonuses, we decided I should be the one to quit my job. I have a background in education. And his job is remote and flexible, meaning he could work from San Diego, whereas mine had to be in-person in LA. When I put in my notice, I cried through it, I\u8217\'20\'19m not ashamed to say that. My boss didn\u8217\'20\'19t make it easy for me because he didn\u8217\'20\'19t want me to go \u8211\'20\'13 I\u8217\'20\'19d worked there for almost 20 years.\par \par \uc2 California allows a relative to be a paid caregiver for family members with disabilities, so that is now my official paid job now \u8211\'20\'13 taking care of Bridget. When I worked as a retail manager, I made $40 an hour. Now I make $14.25, and there\u8217\'20\'19s a cap on the hours. Given that caring for Bridget is a 24/7 job, it would never cover the time we spend. They haven\u8217\'20\'19t raised the hours of care cap, though, even though she is no longer going to school in-person.\par \par \uc2 Recently, a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  memory popped up of me speaking on a panel. I remember saying, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou can have it all, you can have a career, you can have the kids, this company is great and you can make it work\u8221\'20\'1D. I took a lot of pride in that. It made me sad, because I\u8217\'20\'19m not in that same position now.\par \par \uc2 Even though I got a huge sense of identity from the job I left, right now I want to enjoy every morsel of this time with my kids, with the ages that they are at.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thedoubleshift.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Katherine Goldstein}}}\uc2  is a journalist and the creator of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://megaphone.link/DSPRD3546729759"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Double Shift podcast,}}}\uc2  a narrative show that challenges the status quo of motherhood in America. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://megaphone.link/DSPRD3546729759"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Listen and subscribe here }}}\uc2  for an audio series of this story, which begins airing March 10th.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was supported by the Economic Hardship Reporting Project\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210224eh2o001mg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
