\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US minimum wage activists face their toughest foe: Democrat Joe Manchin\b0\par\par\uc2 Zack Harold in Charleston, West Virginia \par \uc2 1,096 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2021\par 17:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Party\u8217\'20\'19s most conservative senator, from one of the poorest states, has advocated for a rise only to $11 an hour \u8211\'20\'13 but workers say it\u8217\'20\'19s not enough\par \par \uc2 Hopes that the US will finally increase the federal minimum wage for the first time in nearly 12 years face a seemingly unlikely opponent: a Democrat senator from one of the poorest states in the union.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin of West Virginia, the state\u8217\'20\'19s former governor and the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 most conservative senator, has long opposed his party\u8217\'20\'19s progressive wing and is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/536977-machin-says-he-doesnt-support-raising-minimum-wage-to-15-per-hour"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on record}}}\uc2  saying he does not support increasing the minimum wage from $7.25 to $15 an hour, the first increase since 2009. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m supportive of basically having something that\u8217\'20\'19s responsible and reasonable,\u8221\'20\'1D he told the Hill. He has advocated for a rise to $11.\par \par \uc2 None of this has found favor with some low-wage workers in a state where an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wvpolicy.org/data-released-today-shows-west-virginia-had-6th-highest-poverty-rate-in-the-country-even-before-covid-hardship-more-relief-needed/#:~:text=An%20estimated%20278%2C734%20West%20Virginians,rate%20among%20the%2050%20states."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated 278,734 West Virginians}}}\uc2  lived in poverty in 2019, 16% of the population and the sixth highest poverty rate in the US.\par \par \uc2 Last Thursday Manchin reaffirmed his stance during a virtual meeting with members of the West Virginia Poor People\u8217\'20\'19s Campaign (WVPPC), a group pushing for an increased minimum wage and other policy changes that would benefit the working class.\par \par \uc2 That meeting was closed to the media but at an online press conference immediately afterward, participants said Manchin refused to budge. \u8220\'20\'1CHe was kind of copping out,\u8221\'20\'1D said WVPPC member Brianna Griffith, a restaurant worker and whitewater rafting guide who, due to exemptions for tipped workers, only makes $2.62 an hour.\par \par \uc2 As a result of her sub-minimum wage job, Griffith received only $67 a week in unemployment benefits until that ran out in August. She lost her house and was forced to move in with her grandmother. Although she has now returned to work, business is slow and she estimates tips have fallen by 75%.\par \par \uc2 When Griffith told Manchin about her plight on Thursday, she said he asked about the $600 stimulus check approved by Congress in December. \u8220\'20\'1CHe seemed to think that $600 \u8230\'20\'26 was enough to get me by,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like he\u8217\'20\'19s got his head in the clouds and he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t understand what\u8217\'20\'19s happening to poor people in West Virginia.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s insistence on an $11 minimum wage, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://livingwage.mit.edu/states/54"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to MIT\u8217\'20\'19s living wage calculator}}}\uc2 , even a $15 minimum wage would only provide a living wage for single West Virginians without children. For a West Virginia family with two working parents and two children, both parents would need to be making at least $20.14 an hour to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 Griffith said if the minimum wage was increased to $15 an hour, \u8220\'20\'1CI could afford to live on my own. I could afford a car that\u8217\'20\'19s not 25 years old.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Rev Dr William Barber, co-chair of the national Poor People\u8217\'20\'19s Campaign, was in last week\u8217\'20\'19s meeting and said Manchin agreed the current $7.25 minimum wage was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot enough\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But Barber said he was \u8220\'20\'1Camazed\u8221\'20\'1D Manchin could hear from people like Griffith and still oppose increasing the minimum wage to $15.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat he is suggesting would just further keep people in poverty and hurting,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Raising the minimum wage was a key part of Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 2020 platform. The former presidential candidate and now Senate budget committee chairman, Bernie Sanders, has referred to the current $7.25 rate as \u8220\'20\'1Ca starvation wage\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The wage hike, formally known as the Raise the Wage Act of 2021, is now part of a proposed $1.9tn Covid-19 relief bill. The measure would incrementally raise the minimum wage from $7.25 to $15 over the next four years.\par \par \uc2 With only a razor-thin majority in the Senate, all 50 Democrat senators need to be onboard for the bill to pass. But in addition to Manchin, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2021/02/12/kyrsten-sinema-democrats-468768"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema of Arizona has told Politico}}}\uc2  she does not want the minimum wage increase to be part of the Covid relief package.\par \par \uc2 There are some reasons to be hesitant about increasing the minimum wage. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbo.gov/system/files/2021-02/56975-Minimum-Wage.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Congressional Budget Office (CBO) report }}}\uc2  detailing the economic impact of the Raise the Wage Act has estimated the legislation would eliminate an estimated 1.4m jobs and would swell the national debt by $54bn over the next decade.\par \par \uc2 But the report also estimates a $15 minimum wage would lift 900,000 people out of poverty nationwide and inject $333m into the US economy.\par \par \uc2 Other economists have disputed the CBO report. Estimates by the left-leaning \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  predict 32 million US workers would benefit from the minimum wage increase, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/minimum-wage-to-15-by-2025-by-congressional-district/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which includes a quarter-million workers in Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s home state of West Virginia}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WVPPC member Pam Garrison was also on Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s call with Manchin. Garrison is 55 years old and says she has earned minimum wage her entire working life and makes ends meet by taking side jobs cleaning houses. She spoke of the mental, physical and emotional toll that living in poverty has on people like her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re just frazzled,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19ve never lived in poverty, you have no idea what it does to you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19ve never lived in poverty, you have no idea what it does to you\par \par \uc2 Pam Garrison\par \par \uc2 Garrison said Manchin \u8216\'20\'18heard our side\u8221\'20\'1D but is reluctant to embrace a $15 minimum wage because he is worried small businesses could not absorb the increased labor costs. But she said giving low-wage workers more money would also benefit small businesses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you give us a decent pay, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to put the money back into the economy [and] we\u8217\'20\'19re going to be able to feed our families,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Members of the WVPPC plan to continue lobbying Manchin on the Raise the Wage Act despite his seeming unwillingness to change his stance on the legislation.\par \par \uc2 The group will hold a masked, socially distanced rally outside his office in Charleston, West Virginia, on Monday. A similar rally will be held at. Sinema\u8217\'20\'19s office in Pheonix, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s office denied multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Zack Harold is a freelance writer and radio producer in Charleston, West Virginia. He is a regular contributor for West Virginia Public Broadcasting\u8217\'20\'19s Inside Appalachia and formerly served as the Charleston Daily Mail\u8217\'20\'19s entertainment editor and managing editor for WV Living, Wonderful West Virginia and WV Focus magazines\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210222eh2m002gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
