\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Some businesses are insisting employees get vaccinated for Covid. Should yours?\b0\par\par\uc2 Gene Marks \par \uc2 777 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2021\par 12:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Whether it means firing workers who refuse, offering incentives or taking a laissez-faire approach, it\u8217\'20\'19s worth having a policy\par \par \uc2 This week a New York City restaurant made news because it allegedly fired an employee because she refused to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/02/17/nyregion/waitress-fired-covid-19-vaccine-refusal.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the New York Times}}}\uc2 , the waitress, who worked in Brooklyn\u8217\'20\'19s Red Hook Tavern, did not want to get a Covid vaccine shot because she was concerned that the vaccine might affect her chances of becoming pregnant in the future. \u8220\'20\'1CI totally support the vaccine,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t for this one thing, I would probably get it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, her bosses saw things differently. The restaurant, which has a policy that requires all of its employees to get vaccinated, terminated the employee. The owner, according to the report, refused to comment on the situation but did say that the restaurant revised its policies to \u8220\'20\'1Cmake it clearer to employees how they could seek an exemption from getting vaccinated\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The situation is one that most businesses \u8211\'20\'13 big and small - are already facing, or will face in the weeks ahead: do we terminate an employee who refuses to get a vaccine? Many companies are choosing this route. Other companies are taking the opposite approach and instead offering incentives to those that get the shot. For example, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/dollar-general-employees-covid-19-vaccine/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dollar General}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.foxbusiness.com/retail/lidl-to-give-extra-200-who-employees-to-get-covid-19-vaccine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lidl}}}\uc2  and others including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aarp.org/work/working-at-50-plus/info-2021/companies-paying-employees-covid-vaccine.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  are actually paying their employees with cash or added time off to get vaccinated. There are even some businesses \u8211\'20\'13 like this innovative restaurant in Detroit \u8211\'20\'13 that are offering {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.freep.com/story/entertainment/dining/2021/02/02/oak-and-reel-restaurant-discount-covid-19-vaccinated-diners/4363204001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 discounts}}}\uc2  to customers who can show they have done the same.\par \par \uc2 Regardless, every company has to address the issue and have a policy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy first piece of advice to employers who require vaccinations is to contact your insurance broker and find out exactly what coverages you have that may apply to a mandatory Covid vaccination policy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Claude Schoenberg, an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://schoenberglawoffice.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 employment attorney}}}\uc2  based in Bala Cynwyd, Pennsylvania. \u8220\'20\'1CYou need to know what protection you might have in case an employee suffers an adverse reaction to a mandated Covid vaccine. You should consider whether that amount of insurance protection is sufficient.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Then there\u8217\'20\'19s the issue of whether just a single one-size-fits-all policy makes sense. Schoenberg is advising his clients to ask themselves whether a Covid vaccination policy would be beneficial to all operations. He suggests considering whether some operations are more safety sensitive than others or if some employees are more at risk than others to contract Covid, based on their work location and proximity to other employees.\par \par \uc2 He also recommends considering what would happen if employees resist vaccination due to their religious leanings or a valid medical reason and also how such a vaccination policy would be implemented. \u8220\'20\'1CWill an employer offer Covid vaccination on premises and at employer cost?\u8221\'20\'1D he asks. \u8220\'20\'1CWill these vaccinations occur during working hours? Will the employer pay employees for time spent receiving a mandatory Covid vaccination?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What do I care if an employee gets vaccinated or not?\par \par \uc2 All of these considerations, and others, need to be taken into account when coming up with your policy. So what happens if you don\u8217\'20\'19t have a policy? That\u8217\'20\'19s a road some of my clients are taking.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat do I care if an employee gets vaccinated or not?\u8221\'20\'1D one owner of a small service company told me this week. \u8220\'20\'1CIf he or she gets sick, that\u8217\'20\'19s their problem. I know I\u8217\'20\'19ll be getting vaccinated so I won\u8217\'20\'19t likely get sick, and neither will others in my office who do the same.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That approach is also valid, as long as he\u8217\'20\'19s prepared to absorb the not insubstantial costs of an employee who has to miss work due to illness. That\u8217\'20\'19s a reason why some employers, like the restaurant owner in Brooklyn I imagine, are requiring vaccinations of their workers.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is that the decision whether or not to require your employees to get vaccinated is still up to the owner of the company. There are no federal guidelines that require Covid vaccinations for workers just like there have been none requiring such vaccinations for the flu or shingles. It\u8217\'20\'19s your workplace so you\u8217\'20\'19re free to choose. But, given the unusual nature of a global pandemic and the ongoing uncertainty of its outcome, it\u8217\'20\'19s still probably a good idea to talk to an expert.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CContact your attorney,\u8221\'20\'1D says Schoenberg. \u8220\'20\'1CYou won\u8217\'20\'19t regret investing time and money obtaining trusted advice and counsel concerning such an important decision.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210221eh2l00107\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
