\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 'I can't keep doing this': gig workers say pay has fallen after California's Prop 22\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,252 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2021\par 23:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Drivers say working conditions remain poor after voters approved measure exempting Uber and other apps from labor laws\par \par \uc2 Weeks after Proposition 22 went into effect in California and exempted some major tech firms from fully complying with labor laws, workers for rideshare and delivery apps in the state claim poor working conditions have persisted and pay has decreased.\par \par \uc2 Drivers and labor groups opposed Prop 22, saying it would allow companies to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2020/oct/29/uber-lyft-racist-business-model-prop-22"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sidestep their obligations}}}\uc2  to provide benefits and standard minimum wages to their workers even as they make billions of dollars. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/04/california-election-voters-prop-22-uber-lyft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the measure passed}}}\uc2  at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s clear that as soon as Prop 22 passed, it was open season to start cutting my pay again,\u8221\'20\'1D said Peter Young, a rideshare driver for four years in Los Angeles. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m looking for other work. I can\u8217\'20\'19t keep doing this at this pay. I\u8217\'20\'19m doing food delivery right now. Everyone is ordering food online so there\u8217\'20\'19s demand. It\u8217\'20\'19s just that what they are choosing to pay me isn\u8217\'20\'19t reliable any more and it\u8217\'20\'19s getting lower.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When the coronavirus pandemic started, Young switched from rideshare driving to relying on food delivery apps such as Uber Eats, \uc2 Grubhub\uc2 , and the Uber-owned \uc2 Postmates\uc2 . After Prop 22 passed, he claimed incentives offered to drivers ahead of the Prop 22 vote disappeared, and he has experienced cuts to his base pay amid unreliable fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you try to earn money, just purely on the delivery fee, it comes out to about $5 an hour. A good day for me is maybe earning $100 before gas and expenses off eight hours of work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Young.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ktla.com/news/california/californias-minimum-wage-set-to-rise-on-jan-1-as-2021-laws-take-effect/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 minimum wage }}}\uc2  in California is $14 an hour as of January 2021, and $13 an hour for employers with less than 25 employees.\par \par \uc2 Ben Valdez has worked part-time as an Uber driver in Los Angeles for five years and continued through the pandemic. He works two to three days per week, 12 to 15 hours per day, and on average makes about $150 per day before expenses, but the pay has continued to vary widely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve had maybe three or four nights where I literally made $4,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Through the pandemic, Valdez has paid for things like face masks and other personal protective equipment out of his own pocket and built his own protective partition in his vehicle out of a shower curtain.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was under the impression that I was going to get an additional $0.30 per mile after Prop 22,\u8221\'20\'1D said Valdez, but he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t received that extra compensation because, according to Uber\u8217\'20\'19s metrics, his pay exceeds their calculation of 120 percent of minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://laborcenter.berkeley.edu/the-uber-lyft-ballot-initiative-guarantees-only-5-64-an-hour-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  by labor economists at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , in October 2019 found Prop 22 guarantees a minimum wage of $5.64 an hour, as only engaged time is accounted for in the wage calculations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of drivers were duped because they expected they were magically going to be able to qualify for benefits that the companies made it sound like they were going to pay for up front and that drivers were going to be getting reimbursement for the mileage,\u8221\'20\'1D said Valdez. \u8220\'20\'1CThey also made drivers believe that if Prop 22 didn\u8217\'20\'19t pass then Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  were going to leave the state of California because they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford to pay drivers as employees.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In November, California voters passed Prop 22, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/California_Proposition_22,_App-Based_Drivers_as_Contractors_and_Labor_Policies_Initiative_(2020)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 58.63%}}}\uc2  of voters in favor of the amendment to exempt app-based gig workers from California assembly bill 5, which granted gig workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/11/california-proposition-22-uber-lyft-doordash-labor-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the rights of employees}}}\uc2  such as unemployment insurance, health insurance, minimum wage, and collective bargaining. Uber, \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , and other gig companies refused to comply with AB5 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2020/aug/19/california-uber-and-lyft-drivers-brace-for-shutdown-over-worker-classification#:~:text=Uber%20and%20Lyft%20threatened%20to,as%20employees%20by%2020%20August."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threatened}}}\uc2  to shut down operations in the state of California if they were forced to do so.\par \par \uc2 Prop 22, authored by Uber, \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , \uc2 Instacart\uc2 , and \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sfexaminer.com/news/uber-rolls-out-fee-for-california-customers-as-prop-22-takes-effect/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went into effect }}}\uc2  in mid-December 2020 after an aggressive public relations campaign of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mercurynews.com/2020/11/16/prop-22-after-200-million-california-brawl-uber-and-lyfts-gig-worker-fight-is-far-from-over#:~:text=The%20two%20ride%2Dhailing%20giants,workers%20are%20employees%20who%20are"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $200m}}}\uc2  launched by the companies. The companies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/11/california-proposition-22-uber-lyft-doordash-labor-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outspent }}}\uc2  opponents to Prop 22 by 10 to one, making it the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abc7.com/22-california-prop-2020-ca-what-is/7585005"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most expensive ballot measure}}}\uc2  in California\u8217\'20\'19s history.\par \par \uc2 Disputes over Prop 22 continue in the wake of its passage.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after it passed, several gig apps {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2020/12/14/tech/uber-prop-22-california-fees/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sfexaminer.com/news/lyft-announces-additional-fee-for-california-riders-to-cover-cost-of-prop-22-driver-benefits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fees}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sf.eater.com/2021/1/20/22240818/postmates-delivery-fees-prop-22"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for customers}}}\uc2  in California would increase to cover the costs of Prop 22 driver benefits after several of the apps {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sf.eater.com/2020/12/15/22176413/uber-eats-doordash-price-hike-fee-december-prop-22"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  prices would hike if Prop 22 didn\u8217\'20\'19t pass.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 California Fair Political Practices Commission{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/v7mj5a/lyft-is-getting-a-slap-on-the-wrist-for-misleading-prop-22-ads"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recently proposed}}}\uc2  a $3,371 fine against \uc2 Lyft\uc2  for deceptive and improperly labeled campaign ads in support of Prop 22.\par \par \uc2 A class action lawsuit was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/uber-class-action-lawsuit-california-prop-22-ab5-driver-pay-2021-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 permitted}}}\uc2  to proceed against Uber, filed by 4,800 drivers demanding back pay and benefits as the rideshare company refused to comply with AB5 before Prop 22 passed. On 10 February, the California supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sfchronicle.com/business/article/California-Supreme-Court-rejects-Uber-Lyft-15941181.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  declined to review}}}\uc2  a lower court ruling against Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  over the classification of their drivers as employees before Prop 22 passed.\par \par \uc2 Rideshare drivers and the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ktla.com/news/california/lawsuit-challenging-prop-22-will-be-refiled-in-alameda-county-after-being-thrown-out-by-ca-supreme-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}\uc2  a lawsuit claiming Prop 22 is unconstitutional because it prevents the California legislature from providing workers\u8217\'20\'19 compensation to workers for the apps.\par \par \uc2 One of the drivers who filed that lawsuit, Saori Okawa, worked as a full-time Uber driver in San Francisco for a year and was also working for \uc2 Instacart\uc2  and \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  when the coronavirus pandemic hit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CProp 22 hasn\u8217\'20\'19t made anything better because the companies still don\u8217\'20\'19t take into consideration the waiting time and driving time to stores, so their guaranteed 120 percent of minimum wage is fraudulent,\u8221\'20\'1D said Okawa.\par \par \uc2 She pays about $180 a week to rent a car through Uber\u8217\'20\'19s partnership with Avis and spends about $90 on gas a week. She works five to six days a week from 7.30am to 6pm. She claimed the gig companies had changed base pay without giving a reason and she was still not provided adequate personal protective equipment while working during the pandemic, or compensated for the time she spent daily disinfecting her vehicle and herself.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m scared and pray I don\u8217\'20\'19t get coronavirus because that means being out of work for weeks without any financial support,\u8221\'20\'1D said Okawa.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for DoorDash said in an email: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re proud that \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  provides flexible earning opportunities for Dashers to supplement their income, and Prop 22 demonstrates that we can pair the independence Dashers want with even greater security. Following the implementation of Prop 22, Dashers in San Francisco are making more than $39 per hour that they\u8217\'20\'19re on a delivery, and Dashers for the first time are able to earn money toward health insurance under this groundbreaking legislation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson also claimed 90% of workers on \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  work fewer than 10 hours per week on the app, and on average dashers deliver less than four hours per week.\par \par \uc2 A Grubhub spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe new benefits from Prop 22 include earnings guarantees, healthcare subsidies and new insurance protections. Overall, Prop 22 ensures benefits for \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  drivers while protecting the flexibility they value. Regarding earnings specifically, drivers in California have more stability now with guaranteed earnings that are based on 120% of the local minimum wage, and total active delivery time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Uber, \uc2 Postmates\uc2 , and \uc2 Instacart\uc2  did not respond to multiple requests for comment on this story.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210218eh2i001mj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*