\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  shifts away from in-person work: 'The 9-to-5 workday is dead'\b0\par\par\uc2 Kari Paul in San Francisco \par \uc2 489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 February 2021\par 21:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Most workers for San Francisco\u8217\'20\'19s biggest private employer will come into an office just one to three days a week\par \par \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  has become the latest tech company in San Francisco to signal a transition away from in-person work, declaring the \u8220\'20\'1C9-to-5 workday is dead\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The city\u8217\'20\'19s largest private employer announced on Tuesday it would permanently allow many employees to work from home, even after it becomes safe to return to offices following the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  said that after polling employees on their preferences, it would transition the majority of workers to a \u8220\'20\'1Cflex\u8221\'20\'1D situation in which they come into an office just one to three days per week. Only a small part of the workforce will continue to work from an office location four or more days per week. Employees who do not live near an office are free to work remotely indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs employers, we have an opportunity to create an even better workplace \u8211\'20\'13 one that allows us to be more connected to each other, find more balance between work and home, and advance equality \u8211\'20\'13 ultimately leading to increased innovation and better business outcomes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brent Hyder, the chief \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople officer\u8221\'20\'1D at \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 , who heads its employee policies.\par \par \uc2 The move follows similar announcements from tech firms {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2020/may/21/facebook-coronavirus-remote-working-policy-extended-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including Facebook}}}\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and has long-term implications for the city of San Francisco, where \uc2 Salesforce{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/sep/25/san-francisco-salesforce-transit-center-transbay-closed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 built a notorious tower}}}\uc2  and transit terminal starting in 2013, making its headquarters the tallest building in the city.\par \par \uc2 During the tech boom in San Francisco in the past decade, companies were known for their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2017/jul/23/inside-billion-dollar-palaces-of-tech-giants-facebook-apple-google-london-california-wealth-power"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sprawling employee campuses}}}\uc2  with perks like cafeterias, fancy on-site gyms, nap pods and games. Those days were over, Hyder suggested.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs we enter a new year, we must continue to go forward with agility, creativity and a beginner\u8217\'20\'19s mind \u8211\'20\'13 and that includes how we cultivate our culture,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAn immersive workspace is no longer limited to a desk in our Towers; the 9-to-5 workday is dead; and the employee experience is about more than ping-pong tables and snacks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Amid the changes to traditional workplace models, many tech professionals {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/01/14/technology/san-francisco-covid-work-moving.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have left the Bay area}}}\uc2 , opting to work from more affordable cities now that jobs provide more flexibility. The most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abc7news.com/tech-leave-the-bay-area-people-move-out-san-francisco-recruiting-programs-big-cities-rent-drop/9792358/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 common destination}}}\uc2  for tech workers leaving the Bay is Austin, Texas, followed by Seattle, New York, and Chicago, according to moveBuddha, a site that compiles data on moving.\par \par \uc2 The exodus of tech talent could have a big impact on the Bay Area, which has been shaped by the tech boom and the money that came with it. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/cities/2019/jul/01/san-francisco-big-tech-workers-industry"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 transformation of the city}}}\uc2  came with gentrification, rising rent costs, increases in homelessness and decreases in diversity. As more high earners leave the city, San Francisco {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sfweekly.com/news/san-francisco-rents-keep-dropping/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has seen rents}}}\uc2  decrease substantially.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210210eh2a003jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
