\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 'I lost everything': laid-off workers battle Florida's chaotic benefit system\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 2,746 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 February 2021\par 17:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Six workers laid off amid the coronavirus pandemic report long delays, backlogs, system crashes and small payments\par \par \uc2 A year since the first {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/26/6/20-0516_article"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 coronavirus case}}}\uc2  was reported in the US, millions of Americans have found themselves out of work for nearly all that time as the pandemic triggered an economic crisis on a scale unseen since the Depression of the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 Florida has been hit harder by the pandemic than nearly any other state and the crisis has wreaked havoc on countless lives, families and communities from the Florida Keys to the Panhandle \u8211\'20\'13 and everywhere in between.\par \par \uc2 The state experienced the second most unemployment claims in the US since the start of the pandemic, with an unemployment increase of 1,683% compared with January last year, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wallethub.com/edu/states-unemployment-claims/72730"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data}}}\uc2  compiled by WalletHub.\par \par \uc2 Florida has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://floridahealthcovid19.gov/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recorded}}}\uc2  over 1.6m cases and more than 25,000 deaths since the start of the pandemic, the third highest case count among states in the US and fourth in total deaths, although its population, at nearly 22 million, is the third largest.\par \par \uc2 Workers who have lost their jobs have been forced to rely on a broken state unemployment system that has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wptv.com/news/state/lawmakers-announce-bill-to-fix-floridas-broken-unemployment-system"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 experienced}}}\uc2  long delays, backlogs, system crashes and small payments as the state\u8217\'20\'19s maximum payout of $275 covers only a portion of the lost income for thousands of workers in the state.\par \par \uc2 Through 2020, Florida was the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wfla.com/community/health/coronavirus/florida-unemployment/florida-was-second-worst-state-in-us-at-paying-unemployment-benefits-on-time/#:~:text=Search%20Search-,Florida%20was%20second%2Dworst%20state%20in%20US,paying%20unemployment%20benefits%20on%20time&text=Only%2057%20percent%20of%20Floridians,only%20state%20worse%20was%20Hawaii."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 second worst state}}}\uc2  in the US at paying unemployment benefits on time. Internal audits had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.firstcoastnews.com/article/news/regional/florida/national-expert-says-floridas-unemployment-system-is-the-worst-in-the-country/77-e0d10f7e-8374-4f0d-9aff-910777981b41"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 uncovered}}}\uc2  various issues with the state\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment system for years before the pandemic hit, and elected officials are still conducting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.palmbeachpost.com/story/news/politics/state/2021/01/08/new-florida-senate-committee-vows-probe-unemployment-system/6587814002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reviews}}}\uc2  on how to fix the state\u8217\'20\'19s ongoing unemployment failures.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  spoke to workers in Florida who have struggled to survive while relying on the state\u8217\'20\'19s broken unemployment system.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of their stories.\par \par \uc2 Ann Largent\par \par \uc2 Former hotel employee at Disney World in Orlando\par \par \uc2 for two years\par \par \uc2 Right before coronavirus shut down Orlando\u8217\'20\'19s tourism industry, Largent was separated from her job of two years at a Walt Disney World hotel, where she worked late-night shifts conducting maintenance and housekeeping duties.\par \par \uc2 Orlando, one of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.voanews.com/usa/still-most-visited-place-orlando-had-75-million-visitors-2018"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most visited tourist destinations}}}\uc2  in the US before the pandemic, has taken {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bizjournals.com/orlando/news/2020/08/21/metro-orlando-worst-in-state-for-year-over-year.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 among the hardest hits}}}\uc2  in job losses as its theme parks, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2020/dec/02/walt-disney-layoffs-workers-struggle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Disney World}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.orlandosentinel.com/business/tourism/os-bz-seaworld-warn-layoffs-20200915-72m2hwjcqzfavjce3irq3mq7ti-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SeaWorld}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.orlandoweekly.com/Blogs/archives/2020/12/04/universal-orlando-announces-layoffs-of-more-than-1000-employees-by-the-end-of-the-year"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Universal Studios}}}\uc2 , and others have permanently laid off thousands of workers.\par \par \uc2 The surrounding area has maintained the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.orlandoweekly.com/Blogs/archives/2020/12/22/orlando-kissimmee-sanford-area-has-the-highest-unemployment-rate-in-the-state-as-floridas-jobless-numbers-hold-steady"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest unemployment rates }}}\uc2  in the state, with an estimated 125,000 jobs lost over 2020.\par \par \uc2 Largent was one of thousands of Floridians who experienced long delays in receiving unemployment benefits through the state. During that time, Largent and her daughter had to leave the house they rented because they could no longer afford it. They moved into a mobile trailer park where they currently reside because rent is too high anywhere elsenearby.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDuring that time frame, I lost everything,\u8221\'20\'1D said Largent. \u8220\'20\'1CCovid-19 has destroyed my life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her car was rear-ended by a truck while she awaited unemployment, and because the driver had no car insurance, she hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been able to repair the damage. Her own car insurance expired last month because she can no longer afford the payments. That puts her driver\u8217\'20\'19s license at risk, which she needs not only in hopes of finding a job, but for taking her 12-year-old daughter, who is currently in remission from cancer, to doctor\u8217\'20\'19s appointments.\par \par \uc2 I lost my health insurance because Medicaid said I was making too much money on unemployment. I only get $247 a week\par \par \uc2 Shortly after she finally started to receive unemployment benefits, her Snap food assistance benefits were cut from $355 a month to $16 a month. Then the federal extended benefits of $600 a week expired on 26 July, leaving Largent and her daughter to survive on just $247 a week, Florida\u8217\'20\'19s maximum unemployment payout after taxes are deducted.\par \par \uc2 After submitting hundreds of job applications, she finally received an offer to start work as a patient care technician at a nursing home, but after working one day was never provided a full schedule.\par \par \uc2 Her unemployment benefits were stopped due to a hold placed on her account for working one day. It took a month for Florida\u8217\'20\'19s department of economic opportunity to remove the hold after weeks of Largent calling for help, spending hours on hold trying to speak with service representatives. While her benefits were on hold, she fell behind on rent.\par \par \uc2 Largent also experienced her own health issues while trying to survive on unemployment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI lost my health insurance because Medicaid said I was making too much money on unemployment. I only get $247 a week. How am I making too much in unemployment? All that pays is my rent,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The health insurance difficulties occurred while Largent required medical attention after she was bitten by a black widow spider on her ankle, and almost lost her foot as a result.\par \par \uc2 Her daughter has struggled through the pandemic, in transitioning to a new, smaller home, struggling with virtual learning and worrying about catching the coronavirus as a cancer survivor with a compromised immune system.\par \par \uc2 Largent explained her daughter\u8217\'20\'19s personality had changed due to the lockdown, from very outgoing and talkative to quiet, reserved and shy.\par \par \uc2 Delaun Stokes\par \par \uc2 Former server at Macaroni Grill in Orlando\par \par \uc2 for two years\par \par \uc2 Stokes worked as a server at Macaroni Grill at Orlando international airport for two years before the coronavirus hit the US and he lost his job.\par \par \uc2 In March, Stokes explained everything started to change suddenly. First tables were cut and spread out to socially distance customers. The next week, Stokes and his colleagues were informed the restaurant was going to be shut down, and he would be furloughed until things returned to normal.\par \par \uc2 By August, his restaurant was still shut and he received a letter informing him that his furlough would be a permanent layoff by 15 October.\par \par \uc2 It went from keeping myself afloat, paying bills, to wondering where I\u8217\'20\'19m going to get my next meal\par \par \uc2 He received unemployment benefits right away unlike thousands in Florida but things got much more difficult when the extra $600 a week of federal extended benefits expired on 26 July.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt went from keeping myself afloat, paying bills, to wondering where I\u8217\'20\'19m going to get my next meal, if I\u8217\'20\'19m going to have enough to pay rent or use what little I had to get food that week, trying to juggle things around got hard,\u8221\'20\'1D Stokes said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe coronavirus relief hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been enough, so we need something more. For the elected officials who are fighting for it, I thank them from the bottom of my heart. For the ones who oppose it, all I have to say is we\u8217\'20\'19ll see you guys next election day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 His mother and stepfather moved out of the area during the pandemic to follow work in audio engineering, so he\u8217\'20\'19s spent the duration of the pandemic without the support system of a family nearby.\par \par \uc2 Stokes and several of his colleagues at Orlando airport have held {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.orlandosentinel.com/news/transportation/os-ne-orlando-airport-workers-protest-20200610-av7lgl7v5nfcxg4tn6ciewiogq-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protests}}}\uc2  to push local elected officials to pass legislation to ensure workers who lost their jobs during the pandemic are provided recall rights to return to their jobs when their workplaces reopen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need assurances to make sure we can go back to our jobs. It\u8217\'20\'19s only fair and it stops us from having to worry about what\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen next,\u8221\'20\'1D Stokes added.\par \par \uc2 Maria E Gonzalez\par \par \uc2 Former cashier at Sbarro\par \par \uc2 in Orlando\par \par \uc2 for four years\par \par \uc2 On 26 March, Gonzalez\u8217\'20\'19s manager at Sbarro pizza assured her the furloughs would be temporary only to receive a letter five months later announcing her layoff would be permanent by 15 October.\par \par \uc2 She\u8217\'20\'19s had several problems with Florida\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment system since she first applied for benefits.\par \par \uc2 When Gonzalez first applied, her application was delayed because she was required to provide her birth certificate after the Florida department of economic opportunity requested further documentation to verify her identity.\par \par \uc2 I call unemployment every single day for the benefits. I\u8217\'20\'19m told they don\u8217\'20\'19t know when I\u8217\'20\'19m going to receive them again\par \par \uc2 In August, Gonzalez worked a temporary job organizing in support of the Biden campaign in Florida, but her job was discontinued after two days because the organization was experiencing technical issues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI reported this to unemployment and they\u8217\'20\'19ve kept me on hold for months, and I\u8217\'20\'19m still on hold five months later,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gonzalez. \u8220\'20\'1CI call unemployment every single day for the benefits. I\u8217\'20\'19m told they don\u8217\'20\'19t know when I\u8217\'20\'19m going to receive them again, they don\u8217\'20\'19t have a time frame, just that they\u8217\'20\'19re fixing it. They said they owe me a lot of money, but they still haven\u8217\'20\'19t given it to me, so I\u8217\'20\'19m having to depend on friends and family to help me with bills because I\u8217\'20\'19ve been behind.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On a daily basis, Gonzalez is harassed by calls from creditors demanding payment, but because of her ongoing hold with unemployment, she\u8217\'20\'19s been unable to pay her bills. Even when she manages to work something out with a service agent for a creditor to defer pay for a month or two, she still receives calls demanding payment while late fees and interest are piling up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m frustrated. I\u8217\'20\'19m depressed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gonzalez. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know what\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen in my future, even in the next month. I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Williams Alvarez\par \par \uc2 Cargo\par \par \uc2 worker at Miami\par \par \uc2 international\par \par \uc2 airport\par \par \uc2 for\par \par \uc2 10 years\par \par \uc2 For 10 years, Alvarez, an immigrant from Cuba, worked as a cargo employee for the contractor Eulen America at Miami international airport.\par \par \uc2 When coronavirus began spreading around the US in March, Williams noted there was immense pressure at work, as personal protective equipment shortages were widespread, and there were ongoing rumors workers would be laid off.\par \par \uc2 On 23 March, Williams received his layoff notice and has tried to survive on unemployment ever since.\par \par \uc2 Like thousands of Americans who were suddenly laid off or furloughed, Williams spent every day for weeks trying to call and log on to unemployment to process his application, but the phone lines were always busy and the website kept crashing.\par \par \uc2 After six weeks, he started receiving benefits, but continued trying to help co-workers secure theirs. During the wait, his bills began piling up, and he\u8217\'20\'19s been behind ever since.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s already been almost a year and I constantly feel like I\u8217\'20\'19m drowning, raising my hand for help, but being ignored\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy car broke down, but I\u8217\'20\'19ve only been able to afford to get part of it fixed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Williams. \u8220\'20\'1CI broke my tooth and had other dental issues, including an infection as a result of dental problems. My uncle passed away, and afterwards responsibilities of caring for my aunt and my mom fell on to me and that\u8217\'20\'19s made it really difficult for me to try to get ahead.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When the federal unemployment benefits of $600 a week expired on 26 July, Williams started falling further behind. He managed to work out a payment plan for his car and mortgage to defer payments for six months, but this February he is expected to restart payments.\par \par \uc2 With the new presidential administration coming in, Williams has some hope things will improve and future coronavirus relief packages will focus on helping the working class and frontline workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis whole pandemic has physically and mentally impacted me. I\u8217\'20\'19m frustrated. I want things to change, but things aren\u8217\'20\'19t changing. It\u8217\'20\'19s already been almost a year and I constantly feel like I\u8217\'20\'19m drowning, raising my hand for help, but being ignored,\u8221\'20\'1D he concluded.\par \par \uc2 Jilma Guevara\par \par \uc2 Cargo security officer at American Airlines subcontractor in Miami\par \par \uc2 for seven years\par \par \uc2 Guevara was shocked when she received a layoff notice on 23 March. For weeks in March there was a lot of insecurity and anxiety about coronavirus, but she had the most seniority in her department, a spotless record, was a backup supervisor, but was the first person laid off in cargo.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt like the roof fell in on my head. I\u8217\'20\'19m diabetic, we were already living through this feeling of anxiety because no one was telling us at work what was going to happen with the pandemic, and then I asked myself, what was I going to do without any money?\u8221\'20\'1D Guevara said. \u8220\'20\'1CI felt humiliated and betrayed. My co-workers and I gave the best of ourselves so we could make \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  and other airlines look good, so to me it was a betrayal, a stab in the back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In October, a congressional subcommittee released a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://coronavirus.house.gov/news/press-releases/new-report-shows-treasury-let-aviation-companies-fire-workers-while-reaping"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  on various airline contractors who misused coronavirus relief funds, as delays in the disbursement of funds allowed contractors to conduct mass layoffs, then subsequently keep payroll relief funds for pre-pandemic jobs.\par \par \uc2 The politicians don\u8217\'20\'19t have any idea what\u8217\'20\'19s happening, their salaries keep coming\par \par \uc2 It took nearly three months before Guevara started receiving unemployment benefits. She relied on former co-workers and friends to help pay for her diabetes medication, food and other bills. She applied for Snap benefits and began applying for other jobs, even though her health issues make her particularly vulnerable to a coronavirus infection.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after she started receiving unemployment benefits, the extra $600 a week of federal extended benefits expired, leaving her with just $1,000 a month in income to cover her rent of $1,575, her medications which cost $400 a month even with reduced costs through a clinic, and for food and other bills.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe politicians don\u8217\'20\'19t have any idea what\u8217\'20\'19s happening, their salaries keep coming. Meanwhile, the average worker, we earn every dollar through our sweat. And either way, life is invaluable. That money helped us stay home and protect ourselves from the pandemic,\u8221\'20\'1D Guevara added.\par \par \uc2 Ramona Vera\par \par \uc2 Airplane cabin cleaner for\par \par \uc2 10 years at Orlando\par \par \uc2 international\par \par \uc2 airport\par \par \uc2 Ramona, 65, and her husband have relied on food banks throughout the pandemic. Their diet has primarily consisted of rice and beans, or whatever food they could afford.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe haven\u8217\'20\'19t been able to eat like we used to eat before. We can\u8217\'20\'19t go out to eat. We can\u8217\'20\'19t buy meat because it\u8217\'20\'19s so expensive,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 When she first lost her job in March, it took about two months to start receiving unemployment benefits after struggling to process her application, and she never received back pay for the weeks of benefits she missed.\par \par \uc2 She receives $500 a month in social security benefits, most of which goes toward her health insurance. While waiting on unemployment, she fell behind on mortgage payments for her mobile home, but managed to get an extension until May 2021. She has no idea how she will pay the back payments she owes.\par \par \uc2 The money we do get immediately goes right back out to pay off bills\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI haven\u8217\'20\'19t been able to pay the mortgage. I\u8217\'20\'19m behind on my credit card bills and my utility bills. I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what I\u8217\'20\'19m going to do right now because it\u8217\'20\'19s been extremely difficult finding work. I\u8217\'20\'19ve been looking everywhere, applying for jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D Ramona said.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been even more difficult trying to find a new job at her age, and she\u8217\'20\'19s frequently tried to get a job at nearby \uc2 Walmart\uc2  stores despite the ongoing risks of coronavirus. Her hopes heading into 2021 are to find a new job so she can afford to survive and to eventually pay off the $106,000 mortgage on her home, which she was paying for three years before the coronavirus hit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBeing without work, and little money coming in, there are a lot of bills and it\u8217\'20\'19s frustrating because there\u8217\'20\'19s no help coming from anywhere. The money we do get immediately goes right back out to pay off bills,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m hoping the mortgage lender gives me some kind of payment plan because right now I can\u8217\'20\'19t pay back the money I owe. They told me I have to pay $7,000 when the extension ends. No way I can pay that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This reporting was supported by a grant from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hillmanfoundation.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sidney Hillman Foundation}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210206eh2600107\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
