\par\par \uc2 Life and style\par \b \uc2 'He wants to work so bad': how the pandemic is affecting US guide dogs and their owners\b0\par\par\uc2 Elizabeth De Luna \par \uc2 1,094 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2021\par 15:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amid lockdowns, their daily activities have been put on hold \u8211\'20\'13 and their owners fear they won\u8217\'20\'19t recover from it\par \par \uc2 The daily life of a working guide dog is filled with dynamic combinations of people, smells, sights and sounds. Their job \u8211\'20\'13 to navigate this cacophony on behalf of their visually impaired handlers \u8211\'20\'13 is intense and important. When Covid-19 hit, these dogs couldn\u8217\'20\'19t open up a laptop and continue working from home. Now, their owners are grappling with the pandemic\u8217\'20\'19s long-term effects on their guides and guardians.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of my very first thoughts when I heard that my city was shutting down was \u8216\'20\'18OK, what am I going to do with my dog?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D says {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/Everything-Guide-Dog-314091712381166"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Marie Villaneda}}}\uc2 , 19, of Bloomington, Indiana. She got her guide, a five-year-old Bernese mountain dog and black lab mix named Milot, at 15. Pre-pandemic, the duo walked 10 miles a day, so \u8220\'20\'1Cit became clear very, very quickly that Milot was bored\u8221\'20\'1D. For guide dogs, \u8220\'20\'1Cguiding is like writing a doctorate dissertation. It takes a lot of the dog\u8217\'20\'19s brain power and physical energy,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, \u8220\'20\'1Cand there\u8217\'20\'19s just nothing equivalent that I can simulate at home.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To combat the mundanity, Villaneda began scouring \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and consulting trainers at the non-profit that trained Milot, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mira.ca/en/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Mira Foundation}}}\uc2 , to learn how to keep his guiding skills sharp. She is now well versed in keeping her dog mentally active. She creates small obstacle courses for Milot in her apartment and plays impulse-control games with him. It keeps him engaged, but it is also a necessity for when the pandemic is over. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want him chasing after a basketball, thinking that somebody wants to play with him when my safety is in his paws,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Villaneda doesn\u8217\'20\'19t foresee having Milot professionally retrained post-pandemic, but she knows she\u8217\'20\'19s unique. \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody I\u8217\'20\'19ve talked to is experiencing significant distractibility within their dogs. A lot of folks are working from home,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, or are preoccupied with priorities that make it difficult to spend time sharpening their guide dog\u8217\'20\'19s skills.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe just wanted to get out and work so bad and obviously you can\u8217\'20\'19t explain to a guide dog what a pandemic is,\u8221\'20\'1D says {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mollyburkeofficial.com/about"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Molly Burke}}}\uc2 , a YouTuber, about her dog Gallop in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=paa7oHuEsus&ab_channel=MollyBurke"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 December video}}}\uc2  that has been viewed more than 900,000 times. For the past six years, Burke and Gallop had travelled the world, navigating airplanes, galas, stages, hotel rooms, and time zone changes. But 10 months into the pandemic, he seemed to give up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the way he got himself through it was to just kind of decide he retired, to assume \u8230\'20\'26 he\u8217\'20\'19s no longer a working dog,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. Her dog was depressed, anxious, and disruptive. The Labernese began stealing food, pulling on walks, and exhibiting other behavior from his pre-working life as a pet.\par \par \uc2 At first, his descent was slow and Burke assumed he might recover when the pandemic ended. But by mid-October, she realized that might not happen. \u8220\'20\'1CI realized he\u8217\'20\'19s continuing to slip, and the world is continuing to stay shut.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Guide dogs usually serve for six to eight years, so Burke knew Gallop\u8217\'20\'19s retirement was on the horizon, but she didn\u8217\'20\'19t expect to find herself stranded without a guide dog so quickly. \u8220\'20\'1CNow I\u8217\'20\'19m just floating around, hoping to God some time in the next three years I get a dog. The emotion of it is huge,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Burke is not alone. There are more than 10,000 guide dog teams in the US; Mira says that the hundreds it works with are all experiencing a need for retraining or early retirement. The burden of dealing with this issue falls on Mira (which is the only organization in the US that provides guide dogs to people ages 11-16) and non-profits like it. Those organizations are often underfunded and volunteer-run, and the added limitations of the pandemic have made it nearly impossible for them to safely train new service animals and retrain those already in service.\par \par \uc2 It has left Burke and other blind and visually impaired Americans in a precarious position for the foreseeable future. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a hot mess,\u8221\'20\'1D says Burke. \u8220\'20\'1CYears of people waiting for new dogs, years of people trying to get retrained \u8230\'20\'26 this is a very real problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A cane offers a mobility alternative, but it would require a significant lifestyle change. With a guide dog, \u8220\'20\'1C[there\u8217\'20\'19s] more freedom to not only move, but to think,\u8221\'20\'1D says Villaneda. At school, \u8220\'20\'1CI can tell Milot that we\u8217\'20\'19re going to math and he will take me to the room on the other side of campus. It\u8217\'20\'19s effortless. A dog allows you to spend a lot more time building your life and less time finding doors or stairs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs a petite, blind woman, living in a big city, I feel exponentially safer having a 100lb dog beside me,\u8221\'20\'1D says Burke. Plus, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe dog is a lot less of a scary symbol of disability. People don\u8217\'20\'19t come up and say, \u8216\'20\'18Oh my God, how old is your cane? What\u8217\'20\'19s its name?\u8217\'20\'19 but all the time people come up to me and say, \u8216\'20\'18Hey, tell me about your dog!\u8217\'20\'19 It opens conversation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But in a pandemic, when well-intentioned strangers approach Villaneda to pet Milot, it makes it harder to maintain social distance \u8211\'20\'13 which of course could be lethal. \u8220\'20\'1CAs somebody who is blind, I don\u8217\'20\'19t notice until they\u8217\'20\'19re up on me. It\u8217\'20\'19s very scary. I\u8217\'20\'19d love to go outside. I want to work my dog. But I\u8217\'20\'19d also like to be alive,\u8221\'20\'1D says Villaneda.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are a vulnerable community [because] we have to touch everything. We need more help from other people,\u8221\'20\'1D says Burke. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of these things put us more at risk and should make our community a top priority [when it comes to vaccination].\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite this, most states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2021/01/13/disabled-coronavirus-vaccine-states/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have not mentioned disabled Americans}}}\uc2  in their plans for a vaccine distribution, and guide dog trainers are not considered essential workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s awful, because an estimated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/disability#:~:text=Results-,One%20billion%20people%2C%20or%2015%25%20of%20the%20world's%20population%2C,million%20people%2C%20experience%20significant%20disabilities."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1 billion people have a disability}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D says Burke, adding that the number grows every day. \u8220\'20\'1CThe disability community is the only minority group that anybody can join at any time in their life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210202eh22001gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
