\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Remote workers are flocking to Hawaii. But is that good for the islands?\b0\par\par\uc2 Brittany Lyte in Kilauea, Hawaii \par \uc2 1,542 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 January 2021\par 15:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 While the influx has been a boon for some, it\u8217\'20\'19s also exacerbated an unaffordable housing market and brought back painful memories\par \par \uc2 Mike McConnell was scared to go outside, even for a walk.\par \par \uc2 After surviving a near-fatal sepsis infection he\u8217\'20\'19d tried to adopt a healthier lifestyle, but it was proving difficult to actualize with the coronavirus surging across his home in San Diego, California.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was kind of like The Walking Dead just trying to avoid everybody,\u8221\'20\'1D said the 53-year-old financial adviser.\par \par \uc2 So McConnell called a realtor in Hawaii, a place he\u8217\'20\'19d only ever been on vacation, and began browsing properties over FaceTime. In October, he bought a $1.2m house on the rural north shore of Kauai without even seeing it in-person.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I landed at the airport it was like, \u8216\'20\'18What did I really buy?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D McConnell said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut the first time I walked in the house I was stoked.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McConnell isn\u8217\'20\'19t alone. The newfound freedoms of remote working have prompted a wave of people to flee the US mainland for the Hawaiian islands. While the total number of relocatees is unknown, signs point towards a major influx. Real estate agents say demand has reached a fever pitch. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://moversandshakas.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scheme}}}\uc2  backed by local business owners to sponsor remote-workers\u8217\'20\'19 flights to Honolulu, in exchange for community service, received more than 50,000 applications for just 50 slots.\par \par \uc2 Some newcomers are making good on a long-held dream, while others are moving rather spontaneously, without knowing how long they will stay. They are coming for the climate, the scenery, the abundance of year-round outdoor activities. Many are fleeing virus hotspots; Hawaii has maintained some of the lowest rates of Covid infections in the nation throughout the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Among them are tech workers from Silicon Valley, couples forced by the pandemic into early retirement and parents seizing the opportunity to enroll their children in private schools offering in-person classes. There are also a number of prominent new Hawaii denizens. Mark Zuckerberg, the Facebook CEO, is riding out the pandemic on Kauai, where he owns a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/jan/17/mark-zuckerberg-hawaii-estate-kauai-land-rights-dispute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 700-acre estate}}}\uc2 . \uc2 Larry Ellison\uc2 , the founder of the software giant \uc2 Oracle\uc2  who owns 98% of Hawaii\u8217\'20\'19s smallest inhabited island of Lanai, announced that he now lives in Hawaii full-time.\par \par \uc2 And while the influx has been a boon for some, it has also increased tensions \u8211\'20\'13 exacerbating a housing market that is already unaffordable for many locals. And, for some, it has brought back painful historical memories of outsiders exploiting the Hawaii lifestyle with little regard for the consequences.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople have been jumping ship and staying in Hawaii since [Captain] Cook,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jonathan Likeke Scheuer, the chairman of the state Land Use Commission. \u8220\'20\'1CSome people will go back and some people will stay. If you really only plan to try to ride out the pandemic here, just please try to do the least amount of damage possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A booming housing market\par \par \uc2 For years Hawaii has lost more residents than it gains. Many people, especially young adults, are driven away by the lack of job prospects, the scarcity of affordable housing, and the fact that Hawaii has the nation\u8217\'20\'19s lowest average wage when adjusted for the exorbitant cost of living.\par \par \uc2 While it\u8217\'20\'19s not yet clear if the wave of new pandemic residents has fully reversed this trend, the evidence of a shift is clearly visible in the housing market, where realtors say demand has soared.\par \par \uc2 In the second half of 2020, single-family homes that would typically take several months or years to sell attracted new buyers in a matter of hours or days, Hawaii realtors say. Bidding wars are now commonplace, driving home sale prices to new heights.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, realtors say properties that would once have been purchased as vacation homes are increasingly being sold as owner-occupied.\par \par \uc2 Virtual house tours are aiding the real estate frenzy, allowing people to make offers on properties, despite travel restrictions and mandatory quarantines, from thousands of miles away.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI had one scenario where these people made a pre-emptive bid way above the asking price on the first day the property hit the market,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Big Island realtor Beth Thoma Robinson. \u8220\'20\'1CI had to tell all the other prospective buyers that if they want to get in the game at all, they would have to get in a huge bidding war.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, some people who grew up in Hawaii but moved to the mainland have been lured back by the ability to \u8220\'20\'1Cwork from wherever\u8221\'20\'1D while enjoying an out-of-state paycheck. Hannah Sirois, a partner at Corcoran Pacific Properties on Kauai, said she recently showed a property to a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  employee who came home to live with family while she looks for her own house.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou do have an influx of people who have Kauai in their soul who lived here before and never really lost that feeling of wanting what life is like here,\u8221\'20\'1D she Sirois said.\par \par \uc2 But while the booming market may be good news for the industry, it is pushing up prices for many local families.\par \par \uc2 Hawaii has the highest proportion of multigenerational homes in the country, with about one in five residents {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hawaiidata.org/news/2020/5/21/are-hawaiis-multigenerational-households-more-at-risk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 living with three or more generations under one roof}}}\uc2 , according to US census data.\par \par \uc2 Tourism job losses resulting from the pandemic\u8217\'20\'19s crippling effect on travel is forcing some Hawaii families to pack even more relatives into already crowded conditions, said Karen Ono, the executive officer of the Kauai Board of Realtors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s this middle gap of people who are leaving the island because they will not be able to get a house because they make too much money to fall in the affordable housing category and they don\u8217\'20\'19t have enough to afford the market price,\u8221\'20\'1D Ono said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe gap is our firefighters, our nurses, our doctors \u8211\'20\'13 all the people that we need here.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18This isn\u8217\'20\'19t a place to escape and drink a Mai Tai\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The arrival of newcomers in Hawaii has a controversial history. The brisk depopulation of the Native Hawaiian people in the early 1800s was in part due to western diseases introduced by white businessmen who forced Hawaiians off their lands to build sprawling pineapple and sugarcane plantations.\par \par \uc2 The decline of Hawaii\u8217\'20\'19s native population during this era \u8211\'20\'13 and the 1893 overthrow of the Hawaiian Kingdom by the \uc2 US government\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 remains a source of intergenerational trauma and anger toward outsiders.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the idea of incentivising Hawaiian escapism during a public health crisis hasn\u8217\'20\'19t gone down well with everyone.\par \par \uc2 Although Hawaii is in the enviable position of having the nation\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/#cases_deathsper100k"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lowest Covid-19 death rate}}}\uc2 , the virus has decimated the tourism industry that buoys the state economy, driving the unemployment rate from the nation\u8217\'20\'19s lowest to its highest. With fewer airplanes coming in, hotels sit empty. An unprecedented number of residents are struggling with food insecurity as they face almost a year of joblessness.\par \par \uc2 So when it comes to moneyed newcomers, some locals are in short supply of the aloha spirit that Hawaii is famous for.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CParticipation in the community is more important than anything and I don\u8217\'20\'19t want to discount that transplant residents or even tourists can be a part of that,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tamara Paltin, a Maui county councilmember. \u8220\'20\'1CBut this is not a place for you to escape to your own solitary world and work behind a computer screen and go to the beach and drink a Mai Tai.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the state also stands to benefit from the crush of new residents, many with plush paychecks, who could help the local economy recover and recoup pandemic-related losses to the state\u8217\'20\'19s tax base.\par \par \uc2 Movers and Shakas, the business-backed program to lure remote workers to Hawaii that saw 50,000 applicants, is banking on precisely that idea.\par \par \uc2 George Yarbrough is the founder of Hub Coworking Hawaii, a co-working space that\u8217\'20\'19s partnering with Movers and Shakas to connect participants with desks. He believes a structured integration of outsiders into the Hawaii community and culture offers a chance to educate them about unique and tightly held customs and values.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t take mangoes from your neighbor\u8217\'20\'19s yard without permission. Remove your shoes before entering a home. Don\u8217\'20\'19t sit your butt on the table of a picnic bench where people eat. Drive slowly. Treat elders with great respect. Don\u8217\'20\'19t show up to a new surf break and steal all the best waves.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDuring the summer we saw more and more new faces out at south shore surf breaks,\u8221\'20\'1D Yarbrough said. \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes people are just clueless.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 So far, McConnell said the reality of living in Hawaii doesn\u8217\'20\'19t stray far from the fantasy. The state\u8217\'20\'19s low virus counts, partly owed to its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://travel.hawaii.gov/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strict pandemic travel rules}}}\uc2 , have allowed him to freely enjoy Hawaii\u8217\'20\'19s pristine, uncrowded beaches and other trademark delights.\par \par \uc2 As he adapts to island life, McConnell said he\u8217\'20\'19s just trying to fit in.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to bring California with me,\u8221\'20\'1D McConnell said. \u8220\'20\'1CI like it here the way it is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210127eh1r001xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
