\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US companies using pandemic as a tool to break unions, workers claim\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato in Florida \par \uc2 1,297 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 January 2021\par 09:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employers are alleged to be leveraging coronavirus-induced unemployment to mount an assault on collective bargaining\par \par \uc2 Dalroy Connell has worked as a stagehand for the Portland Trailblazers since 1995 when the basketball team began playing games at the Rose Garden Arena. When the pandemic hit the US in March 2020, public events were shut down and \uc2 NBA\uc2  games were briefly suspended before the season moved to a \u8220\'20\'1Cbubble\u8221\'20\'1D in Orlando, Florida, and the season {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.insider.com/nba-disney-bubble-when-it-starts-how-to-watch-2020-6#:~:text=The%20NBA%20will%20resume%20its,strict%20health%20and%20safety%20protocols."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  recommenced}}}\uc2  without fans in July 2020.\par \par \uc2 Connell and his colleagues have been on unemployment ever since, but when the 2020-2021 \uc2 NBA\uc2  season began in December 2020, instead of bringing back several of these workers, the Portland Trailblazers replaced most of the unionized crew who work their games with non-union workers, even as their jobs running the sound and lighting equipment are required whether or not fans are in attendance.\par \par \uc2 Like many workers around the US Connell believes he has been locked out from his job by a company that has used the coronavirus pandemic as a tool to break unions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a blatant slap in the face,\u8221\'20\'1D said Connell. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re using positions in the house, people who already work there to do things we normally do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The workers\u8217\'20\'19 union, International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees (IATSE) Local 28, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/19-RC-270150"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  filed }}}\uc2  unfair labor practice charges with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  and held {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/IATSE.Local28/posts/3432819036767701"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protests }}}\uc2  outside of Portland Trailblazers home games.\par \par \uc2 Connell alleged management at the Portland Trailblazers has frequently fought the union over the past several years, with the latest refusal to recall union workers an extension of this trend.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHere we are wasting a ton of money on legal fees just to give a few guys some work. It\u8217\'20\'19s a five-hour job. It\u8217\'20\'19s so easy to work this out,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 The Portland Trailblazers and Rip City Management did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Lockouts are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tcf.org/content/report/time-courts-end-labor-lockouts/?session=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 work stoppages}}}\uc2  initiated by the employer in a labor dispute where the employer uses replacement workers.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the pandemic, some employers resorted to conducting mass layoffs of workers after union organizing drives surfaced, such as at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-07-19/baristas-lose-jobs-covid-19-unionization-augies-coffee-house"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Augie\u8217\'20\'19s Coffee Shop}}}\uc2  in California and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/federal-charges-filed-by-teamsters-against-warren-buffett-owned-furniture-firm-for-mass-firing-of-coronavirus-whistleblowers-union-supporters-301034484.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cort Furniture}}}\uc2  in New Jersey. Several workers have claimed they had been fired in retaliation due to worker organizing efforts by employers such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2020/may/05/amazon-protests-union-organizing-cracking-down-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/apr/23/labor-unions-trader-joes-workers-coronavirus-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  and most recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ufcw.org/wp-content/blogs.dir/61/files/2021/01/Jan-2021-Instacart-Fires-Nearly-2000-Grocery-Workers.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Instacart}}}\uc2 . Now some employers are beginning to use lockouts as a tactic to seemingly suppress organizing efforts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLockouts are an economic weapon employers use to take the initiative in collective bargaining,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alex Colvin, dean of the school of industrial and labor relations at Cornell University. \u8220\'20\'1CDuring the pandemic, lockouts pose a greater threat to unions due to the high unemployment rate and greater availability of replacement workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/bloomberg-law-analysis/analysis-union-employers-have-no-lockout-card-to-play-for-now"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis by Bloomberg Law}}}\uc2 , no employer lockouts were conducted in 2020 during the first several months of the pandemic, but after previous economic recessions in the US, lockouts rebounded as disputes over wages and benefits became more intense.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe intent of many lockouts is to actually try to break the workers\u8217\'20\'19 unions by showing that the union\u8217\'20\'19s position has led to the loss of work, and the only way to restore work is through unconditional surrender,\u8221\'20\'1D said Moshe Z Marvit, a labor and employment lawyer and fellow at the \uc2 Century Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Los Angeles, California, dozens of workers at Valley Fruit and Produce represented by \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  Local 630 went on strike in May 2020 in protest of intimidation of union members and efforts to decertify the union during new contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Amid negotiations to end the strike and bring back workers, Valley Fruit and Produce replaced several workers with non-union members, while the union alleges workers who did return to work were coerced into signing declarations against the union.\par \par \uc2 The union is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/21-CA-267121"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  currently pursuing }}}\uc2  unfair labor practice charges with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  for the produce distribution company circumventing the union to directly negotiate with workers, in addition to several allegations of intimidation and harassment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThrough their union buster lawyer, Valley Fruit talked to foremen to call workers on the picket line, using intimidation and scare tactics to get them back to work., When workers went back inside, they were forced to sign documents to say they didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to be a part of the union any more,\u8221\'20\'1D said Carlos Santamaria, divisional representative for Teamsters Local 630.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m disappointed in what Valley has done to all the workers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rene Gomez, who worked at Valley Fruit and Produce for 21 years and has been locked out of his job since last year. \u8220\'20\'1CMy family and I are having a hard time economically because of everything going on. We\u8217\'20\'19ve gone to food banks. We\u8217\'20\'19ve been stressed because we don\u8217\'20\'19t know how we\u8217\'20\'19re going to keep paying rent at the end of the month.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Roberto Juarez, who has worked for Valley Fruit and Produce for six years before getting locked out of his job, argued the company has attempted to \u8220\'20\'1Cdestroy the union in the workplace\u8221\'20\'1D, through negotiating in bad faith by pushing for reduction in benefits, wage freezes, hiring union avoidance attorneys, while receiving between {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pppreport.org/company/val+pro+inc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $2m and $5m}}}\uc2  in paycheck protection program loans from the federal government.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the pandemic started and hit hard, we never stopped working and we were working a lot of hours. We were exposing ourselves, coming to work, exposing our families, and they didn\u8217\'20\'19t really care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Juarez.\par \par \uc2 Valley Fruit and Produce did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month in Chicago, Illinois, the Chicago Public Schools district began locking out dozens of teachers who are being ordered to return in-person to schools, which have been conducting virtual learning since the pandemic hit the US in March 2020.\par \par \uc2 Kirstin Roberts, a preschool teacher at Brentano elementary math and science academy in Chicago, refused to return to in-person teaching due to unsafe working conditions, even as all of her students had opted to continue remote learning.\par \par \uc2 The city of Chicago {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abc7chicago.com/chicago-covid-stay-at-home-advisory-lightfoot-extends-order-lori/9541263/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remains}}}\uc2  under a stay-at-home advisory with travel restrictions in place to try to mitigate the spread of coronavirus. Chicago Public Schools {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ctulocal1.org/posts/630a-today-ctu-teachers-decry-virtual-lockout-as-more-covid-cases-surface-in-schools/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threatened}}}\uc2  to declare teachers ordered to work in school buildings who do not show up as \u8220\'20\'1Cabsent without leave\u8221\'20\'1D, and docking their pay.\par \par \uc2 Teachers across Chicago and the \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  held a virtual teach-in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abc7chicago.com/chicago-public-schools-ctu-cps-board-of-education-president/9623653/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protest}}}\uc2  of a return to in-person teaching outside of the board of education president\u8217\'20\'19s home on 13 January.\par \par \uc2 Roberts attended the protest and taught on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  live because she was locked out of her Chicago Public Schools Google account, banning her access to continue teaching her students remotely and shutting her out of her work email account.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ve been trying to impose conditions on the workforce without input from the union,\u8221\'20\'1D said Roberts. \u8220\'20\'1CEven though we\u8217\'20\'19re in the middle of a pandemic, Chicago Public Schools is willing to use our students, hurt our students, and deny students things they need like access to their teachers in a game to one-up the \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  and that\u8217\'20\'19s ridiculous.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to Chicago Public Schools, 87 teachers and staff are currently considered absent without leave, with an attendance rate of about 76% of school district employees in attendance who were expected to return to work in-person, not including employees who had an approved accommodation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are grateful to the teachers and school-based staff who have returned to their classrooms, and we are continuing to meet regularly with the \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  (CTU),\u8221\'20\'1D said a spokesperson for Chicago Public Schools in an email.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210126eh1q00131\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
