\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 What can Biden do to reverse Trump's assault on labor rights?\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,418 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2021\par 18:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 With Covid-19 still raging across the US, job safety experts say the president-elect must issue nationwide regulations directing businesses to protect their workers\par \par \uc2 Soon after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is inaugurated as the next US president, he is expected to take several strong steps on the labor front aimed at reversing or remedying the policies of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who promised workers a lot and delivered little.\par \par \uc2 But Biden\u8217\'20\'19s number one priority will be to tackle the issues left in the wake of the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s nominee for Secretary of Labor, \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the Mayor of Boston and before that a labor leader for decades \u8211\'20\'13 is likely to help advance Biden\u8217\'20\'19s promises to help unions expand. Walsh, who had headed Boston\u8217\'20\'19s federation of building trades unions, is expected to support increased spending on infrastructure to upgrade roads and bridges, strengthen the economy and create good-paying jobs. As mayor, he pushed for construction contractors to hire more workers of color.\par \par \uc2 With Covid-19 still raging across the US, many job safety experts say it\u8217\'20\'19s urgent for Biden to do something that the Trump administration has failed to do: issue nationwide regulations directing businesses to take specific steps to protect their workers from the coronavirus, whether retail, restaurant, factory or construction workers.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s business-friendly \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration (Osha) \uc2 repeatedly rejected labor unions\u8217\'20\'19 pleas to adopt such safety standards.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CClearly priority one is an emergency standard for Osha dealing with Covid and equally a focused, full-on press on inspections, enforcement and guidance. It means doing everything Osha hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been doing,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 David Weil\uc2 , a top labor department official under \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CUltimately the only way we\u8217\'20\'19ll get public safety and reopening is if we get worker safety.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Weil, now the dean at Brandeis University\u8217\'20\'19s Heller School for Social Policy, said Osha under Biden should get far tougher than Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Osha in holding employers accountable for failing to take steps to minimize Covid. Many worker advocates were dismayed by the modest fines that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Osha assessed, for instance, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.duluthnewstribune.com/newsmd/coronavirus/6655554-OSHA-fines-Smithfield-Foods-13494-for-not-protecting-South-Dakota-workers-from-COVID-19-faces-backlash-from-company-and-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $13,494 penalty against Smithfield\u8217\'20\'19s pork-processing plant in Sioux Falls}}}\uc2 , South Dakota, where at least 1,294 workers contracted Covid. Smithfield\u8217\'20\'19s parent company had revenues of $24.1bn last year.\par \par \uc2 Biden has signaled he will take many other pro-worker actions.\par \par \uc2 Although Trump held himself out as a champion of workers, he did nothing to raise the minimum wage or enact paid leave, while Biden has repeatedly called for a $15 minimum wage and guaranteeing workers 12 weeks\u8217\'20\'19 paid family and medical leave. Biden has also backed far-reaching legislation that would make it easier to unionize, and his appointees to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) are likely to reverse many Trump labor board decisions that made it harder for workers to join unions.\par \par \uc2 Steven Pitts, a longtime labor economist at the University of California, Berkeley, said Biden should pursue two sets of labor policies. \u8220\'20\'1COne is try to raise and protect labor standards like a higher minimum wage and tougher safety rules,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CSecond is to build worker power into policy. Too often we focus on the former and not the latter.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden has indicated he will seek to build worker power by strengthening unions. On election day eve, campaigning in western Pennsylvania, he promised to be \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.post-gazette.com/local/region/2020/11/02/Joe-Biden-reaches-out-laborers-unions-campaign-event-Beaver-County-election-vote/stories/202011020106"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the most pro-union president you\u8217\'20\'19ve ever seen}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://joebiden.com/empowerworkers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his campaign platform}}}\uc2 , Biden endorsed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edlabor.house.gov/imo/media/doc/PRO%20ACT%20-%20Fact%20Sheet.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Protecting the Right to Organize Act (Pro Act)}}}\uc2  which would make it easier for unions to organize workers by, among other things, greatly increasing penalties on corporations that break the law in battling to keep out unions.\par \par \uc2 Solid Republican opposition, along with a filibuster, blocked efforts by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  to enact pro-union legislation, and it will be similarly difficult for Biden to enact the Pro Act unless he musters 60 votes to overcome a filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Because Senate Republicans so often vote in line with corporate America\u8217\'20\'19s wishes, it might also be difficult for Biden to get a $15 minimum wage or paid family and medical leave through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 William Samuel, director of government affairs at AFL-CIO, the largest federation of unions in the US, acknowledges that passing the Pro Act \u8211\'20\'13 a huge goal of labor \u8211\'20\'13 won\u8217\'20\'19t be easy. He recommends that Biden, in addition to pushing to pass the Pro Act, pursue a parallel strategy: \u8220\'20\'1CHe should use his bully pulpit to support the right of workers to organize and to shame employers who trample on those rights\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 for example, by criticizing \uc2 Amazon\uc2  for firing the worker who led a New York walkout over Covid-19 safety.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere will be lots of obstacles in his [Biden\u8217\'20\'19s] way and we can hope and pray he can use his executive authority and the bully pulpit to advance the cause of workers,\u8221\'20\'1D Samuel said.\par \par \uc2 Because the Senate might be a formidable barrier to pro-union or pro-worker legislation, Samuel says Biden should use federal procurement policy to raise standards for workers. Many worker advocates want Biden to require federal contractors to pay their workers a minimum of $15 an hour and guarantee them twelve-weeks\u8217\'20\'19 paid medical and family leave.\par \par \uc2 Biden and his running mate, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , opposed a California ballot initiative in which voters \u8211\'20\'13 swayed in part by a $200m campaign financed by Uber and other app-based companies \u8211\'20\'13 approved a measure that categorizes Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers and \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  delivery workers as independent contractors rather than employees. By defining them as independent contractors, the ballot initiative, known as Proposition 22, means these workers won\u8217\'20\'19t receive state minimum wage or overtime protections, won\u8217\'20\'19t receive paid sick days or unemployment compensation and won\u8217\'20\'19t have their companies reimburse their expenses, like gasoline.\par \par \uc2 Uber has said it hopes to pass Prop 22-like laws or ballot initiatives to other states. But Pitts said: \u8220\'20\'1CHow do we find a way to stop the spread of Prop 22 around the country \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s an important thing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a slog to undo all the damage that\u8217\'20\'19s been done the past four years by the Trump majority on the board\par \par \uc2 Wilma Liebman\par \par \uc2 Worker advocates hope Biden\u8217\'20\'19s labor department will issue guidance that goes far to trump Prop 22 by saying that under federal minimum wage and overtime laws, Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers should be considered employees, not independent contractors, because they are not truly independent: Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have great control over the work the drivers do, and the drivers are not really independently in business for themselves.\par \par \uc2 Weil, who headed the labor department\u8217\'20\'19s wage and hour administration, said Prop 22\u8217\'20\'19s definition of independent contractors contradicts the definition under the federal Fair Labor Standards Act, a 1938 law that creates nationwide minimum wage and overtime protections. \u8220\'20\'1CYou just can\u8217\'20\'19t decide, \u8216\'20\'18I can treat them as independent contractors if that\u8217\'20\'19s counter to what our Fair Labor Standards Act says,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Weil said.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-lyft-uber-contract/u-s-federal-government-awards-transportation-contract-to-uber-lyft-idUSKBN2831S5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 awarded an $810m contract to Uber and Lyft}}}\uc2  to provide rides to public agencies. Worker advocates say the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  should use its power to set contract conditions to insist that Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  pay their drivers $15 an hour and treat them as employees.\par \par \uc2 Labor leaders say they hope Biden\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 NLRB\uc2  will reverse many of the anti-union actions taken by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 NLRB\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 for instance, making it harder for workers at franchises like \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  to unionize, making it harder for small groups of workers at larger establishments to unionize, letting employers prohibit workers from using the company email system to discuss union matters, letting companies bar union organizers from spaces open to the public, like corporate cafeterias.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a slog to undo all the damage that\u8217\'20\'19s been done the past four years by the Trump majority on the board,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wilma Liebman, who was NLRB chair during Obama\u8217\'20\'19s first term. \u8220\'20\'1CTheir overruling of precedent has been so sweeping. They\u8217\'20\'19ve overruled precedents that went back decades.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Liebman said the Trump board\u8217\'20\'19s reversals of precedent twisted a pro-worker law, the National Labor Relations Act, in ways that always favored employers. But Republicans say the Trump board was merely undoing what they saw as the unduly pro-union tilt of the Obama board.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210109eh190012z\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbr